{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1549137-renown-rank-a-deep-dive","id":1549137,"title":"Renown & Rank, a Deep Dive","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2025-03-10T15:02:54-07:00","id":1549137,"title":"Renown & Rank, a Deep Dive","content":"<span style=\"font-size:14px\"><u><b>What is Renown?</b></u></span><br /> <br /> Renown reflects how well a Garou meets the expectations of society. It's a mechanical and narrative element which ties together all of Garou society, contributing to changes in Rank.<br /> <br /> Renown is split into permanent dots (rarely changing) and temporary points (constantly in flux). There are three kinds of Renown, Glory, Honour and Wisdom.<br /> <br /> The systems of Renown, like I say, are both mechanical and narrative. Much like becoming Prince in VTM, or ascending through the Nobility in CTD, are bound by politics and monstrous actions, WTA can at times be far more similar than people expect. There is backstabbing, infighting, and actions which would make for a poor leader in a sane society may well be rewarded in this one. All this to say, if you find yourself looking at the Renown system and thinking that it's unfair, corrupt, riddled with holes or fundamentally unfair, I would call that a Feature, not a Bug, of the setting.<br /> <br /> Because Garou are heroes, albeit monstrous ones, you will see quite a lot of defenses of the Renown system, and associated elements like the Litany and Rank. Just to get it out of the way: criticism of the Garou Nation, and Garou, is a fundamental aspect of the source material. Garou society is explicitly framed as unjust and having deep problems, plenty of which are of their own making. None of that is a criticism of the game, or indeed your characters past and present. I'd argue that it's part of what makes them great!<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\"><u><b>Is Renown Objective?</b></u></span><br /> <br /> In a word, no. Much as any decision a government makes is inherently political, any award or removal of Renown is based on values and judgements which come down to the group or individual. If you don't believe me, look at extremist Camps.<br /> <br /> Do Mother's Fundamentalists, reporting to Fundamentalist Elders, reciting their deeds furthering the Fundamentalist cause, suffer losses of Renown? Not at all. Actions which would be dishonourable in a mainstream Sept may be &quot;mission complete&quot; elsewhere.<br /> <br /> You'll see something similar with Tribes. What targets are considered 'glorious' to destroy will depend on the tribal majority. The Get of Fenris consider Sidhe to be their ancient enemies. The Red Talons have great antipathy for humans as a species. Silver Fangs might see any unruly Pack, Camp or Sept, or even Kingdom, as a valid target for warfare, to push their claim to rule over the entire Garou race.<br /> <br /> There is, however, also the matter of incomplete information. W20 establishes that the spirit world will witness your acts even if other Garou are unaware of them. If you break the Litany, for example, you will lose Renown even if other Garou never find out. If you pretend to have dishonoured yourself for honourable reasons, such as pretending a Metis cub is yours in order to protect Gaia in some fashion, the Storyteller may well rule that the Honour on your sheet winds up being better than what your fellows believe of you. Whether a Garou framed of some crime loses Renown or not is also a nebulous area, but given that loss of Rank and Renown can be forced by Punishment Rites and the actions of others, it's clear that framing can have tangible results for the dots on your sheet.<br /> <br /> So, here we see an apparent contradiction, at least at first glance. We know for a fact that there are Elders in every Tribe. We also know that Garou of Ranks 4-5 are an expected feature of a Sept. So there is a presumably consistent (we'll get to that!) spiritual observation of Garou behaviour which is part of the flow of Renown. There is an inconsistent political observation of Garou behaviour which is part of the flow of Renown. We can only assume that the latter is influential, or takes the driver's seat, since if it were secondary to the opinion of the spirits we would see the Renown of certain groups 'lagging' due to their dishonourable or unwise behaviour.<br /> <br /> It's possible to resolve this however! The mistake is to assume there is anything homogeneous about spiritual judgement of a Garou's actions. Ancestor Spirits vary massively between Tribe, Auspice, Camp and so on. A Mother's Fundamentalist who secretly assassinates a Kinfolk patriarch of a rival Fianna family may well gain Honour since the opinion of their own warped Ancestors, and the Red Stag, matter more to their spiritual standing than others.<br /> <br /> Subjectivity is also baked into many of the reward examples themselves. Defend a criminal who is proven guilty? Dishonourable. It actually turns out they're innocent? Retroactively, what you did is now honourable. So your status is determined by the ultimate verdict! How things &quot;come out in the wash&quot;, whether a strategy works or not, whether an accusation lands or not, whether a scheme pans out, play a big part. Which means that Renown is, in part, for winners, and in a more meta sense for those who have good luck when rolling d10s. That brings in issues of spin and public relations.<br /> <br /> W20 does also highlight that the Renown expectations will vary depending on Auspice and Tribe. A Ragabash can get away with certain lies and tricks since it's expected, but by the same measure they may struggle to gain Honour they rightly deserve at times. A Fianna's bardic works might make a bigger splash Renown wise than those of a Glass Walker, who comes from a Tribe which places relative emphasis on genius and innovation over storytelling. The reverse might be true if a Fianna discovers something of technological interest!<br /> <br /> So, all this to say, Renown isn't objective. There's a complex interplay of spiritual, legal, political, cultural and contextual factors at play. That is good and interesting, and helps to feed into themes of Personal Horror. And if you find yourself balking at what I've written, just keep in mind that there is one thing we don't see in WTA, to the best of my knowledge. Very experienced, very successful Garou who are beloved by everyone in their Sept and who have many, many victories under their belt, but who are stuck at Rank 1-2 with no clear explanation. If Renown is objective, fixed or fair in some way which Garou fail to understand, if it reflects some pure and inalienable Gaian reality, then you would expect to find Garou who keep putting out hit after hit but never get their flowers because their Totem, or Ancestors, or perhaps a force beyond them, knows that at their heart they are truly lacking in Honour or Wisdom. The observable reality of WTA is that Garou who get on well, and are seen to succeed, accumulate Renown.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\"><u><b>Glory</b></u></span><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I shall be valorous<br /> I shall be dependable<br /> I shall be generous<br /> I shall protect the weak<br /> I shall slay the Wyrm </div> </div> </div>Glory is gained largely through destroying the enemies of the Garou, especially those that serve the Wyrm. Victories which are clean and impressive are particularly valued. Galliards and Ahroun, the Auspices with the highest Rage, have the strongest tie to Glory as it is the major determinant in their rise in Rank.<br /> <br /> This is arguably the simplest category of Renown. Garou are a warrior culture, they see themselves as Gaia's ultimate weapons, and so the only pertinent debates will be over what is an ally and what is a foe. How much Glory was awarded in the War of Rage? How much in the War of Shame? When the Elder Brothers were maligned as being 'of the Wyrm' due to ignorance, were the Garou who conquered their Caerns and unwittingly released Banes kept imprisoned for centuries found wanting in their Glory?<br /> <br /> Given that Garou society is monstrous and historically they have made many terrible decisions, I would argue that it is more likely that Renown was reinforcing this cycle rather than slowing it down. If the invasion of North America by European Garou was failing to pay dividends in Glory due to spiritual disapproval then I find it unthinkable that this would go unmentioned. My takeaway would be that if you and your Sept believe your enemies were 'of the Wyrm' chances are you'll be getting enough Glory that you think you're right due to the actions of your fellow Garou. The function of various Rites and Renown Rewards serve to muddy the waters enough that even if spirits were curbing your rewards you likely wouldn't notice, and to be honest I'm not sure that they would have been in many cases.<br /> <br /> A core problem with Glory is that it rewards making enemies, so long as you don't appear outright foolhardy. It rewards violent solutions over peaceful ones. It rewards behaving in a way which leads to others Challenging you, so long as you are sure you will win. After all, you <i>do</i> typically get to pick the competition when you are challenged. It rewards bragging, and having the gift of the gab, and having access to things that need killing. All of this forms a series of what we call 'perverse incentives', where the intended function of a reward doesn't match with the actual incentives it creates. Might does make Right for Garou, so the fastest way to accumulate Glory is to be the strongest you can possibly be in your &quot;Challenge bracket&quot; (1/2, 1/2/3, 2/3/4, 3/4/5, 4/5).<br /> <br /> Glory is lost by picking battles you can't win, unless this forms a heroic sacrifice. It is also lost for cowardice (including perceived cowardice!) as well as costly defeats. It's important to note that there is every chance you could accumulate Glory for quite a long time without ever truly being tested. It falls to the Storyteller, or perhaps an Ahroun of greater Rank, to watch for this. Circumstance might see an Ahroun repeatedly confronted with enemies who though theoretically fearsome were never any threat to them. And fighting something which is no threat to you does not show the courage one only finds when their life is on the line.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\"><u><b>Honour</b></u></span><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I shall be respectful<br /> I shall be loyal<br /> I shall be just<br /> I shall live by my word<br /> I shall accept all fair challenges </div> </div> </div>While there are many ways to gain Honour, many rewards for this trait are tied to fulfilling the tasks which are expected of Garou. Protecting wolves, Kinfolk and Garou. Being honest. Acting as a mentor. Honour is the core measure of a Philodox, most especially. Theurges and Ahroun need very little Honour at first, but suddenly find themselves requiring huge amounts to access the uppermost Ranks.<br /> <br /> There's something important to keep in mind about Honour, however. It's Garou Honour. To a Garou it may be honourable to engage in murder of the innocent (to protect the Veil, Litany or Gaia), to perform acts which most humans would consider unseemly or barbaric, and to uphold a system which is, to be frank, deeply dishonourable.<br /> <br /> The Garou Nation believes that Might Makes Right. It believes that it is dishonourable to allow your loved ones to 'suffer' your sickness and disability. It encourages its members to commit ritual suicide. In many Septs the grasp on bodily autonomy and consent is very tenuous. Changing Ways gives an example of an Alpha who constantly belittles and mocks the only other Garou in the pack who could possibly challenge her, making her the hazing victim of the entire Pack, talking her down at every opportunity, bullying and even attacking her. We have a canon example of this victim going to various people for help and basically being told, with varying levels of sympathy, that might makes right and that if she wants this to stop she should either leave the Pack or Challenge her abuser... a fight she will likely lose given that her confidence and life path have been so thoroughly wrecked by the person she would be fighting. This isn't presented as an abnormal situation.<br /> <br /> Basically when you're working with Honour Renown, forget everything you know about Honour. Most of the time, &quot;ah, but if you <i>really</i> think about it, isn't it more honourable to-&quot; won't fly. The exceptions we're shown are exceptions. The Children of Gaia are exceptions. The Glass Walkers are exceptions. The Bone Gnawers are exceptions. The Black Furies are exceptions. Your favourite canon NPC is an exception. And each of them gets things right (from a human perspective) which the others get wrong. A Child of Gaia may have the right idea about not punishing a victim of rape or abuse, but have missed the memo on vaccines. A Philodox may rescue Kinfolk from domestic and child abuse but consider their abusers to be fundamentally righteous, even if they're taking their rights 'too far'.<br /> <br /> A tension between what is truly honourable (interfering to protect a victim from a bully) and what is Honourable (advising the victim to either fight back or shut up) makes for great drama. Are you going to rage against the machine, or rage against those who are stopping it from working with their constant moaning about what's 'fair'. Honour is best explored in a Chronicle where both the Storyteller and the players remember that the 'Honour' of an ancient warrior culture is somewhat alien to us. It's also important to remember that there are many, many things which you'd imagine would cost you Honour but don't. Bullying a packmate doesn't cost you Honour by default, so long as you do not talk down your pack as a whole.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\"><u><b>Wisdom</b></u></span><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I shall be calm<br /> I shall be prudent<br /> I shall be merciful<br /> I shall think before I act and listen before I think </div> </div> </div>Possibly the most controversial given the Garou Nation's track record with rash and unwise behaviour. W20 makes a point to say that Glory and Wisdom are often at odds with one another in Garou, and that mastering both takes something pretty special. Glory is often awarded most generously when you pick every battle you possibly can, so long as those risks pay off. Wisdom is at times awarded for taking the high road, going to the root of the problem, outwitting a foe instead of destroying them, or preventing battle from being necessary in the first place.<br /> <br /> You can get Wisdom for purification, for uncovering the Wyrm, for understanding and following the spirits. Problems do arise however. Much as what a Garou considers Honourable may not be very honourable, what the Garou consider Wise may not be all that wise. Metis lose Wisdom for keeping their deformity hidden. Essentially, the state mandates that if you refuse to be a victim of prejudice, you are showing a lack of wisdom. Making use of modern methods which may show great wisdom can cost you Wisdom. You're asked to take part in a Rite, but you know of a reason it would be a poor decision? Well you'd better take part, as ordered, or lose some Wisdom. You're offered a promotion out of favouritism? Turning it down is unwise.<br /> <br /> Frenzy, a result of circumstance in most cases, costs you Wisdom. Which makes Ahroun rather unwise for being born on the full moon, various Garou unwise for being attacked by a Bitter Rage, and even more Garou unwise for existing in a culture where hazing and bullying are relatively common.<br /> <br /> Meanwhile it's relatively easy, if you have the favour of a Theurge mentor, to gradually work your way through learning the various low-level Rites and farm up Renown. Now of course the ST should arbitrate here. Just as it isn't impressive for a Rank 4 Ahroun to slay a group of weak Fomori, and thus the Glory reward should be minimal if any, it also isn't very impressive for a Theurge who is a quick reader to master their 10th Level 1 Rite of the year. But it does stand that learning these Rites makes you more 'Wise'. Now I'm sure that there is wisdom in taking time for such things, and there is wisdom in the lessons you learn from performing these Rites for the first time... but ultimately you can substitute demonstrating true and enduring wisdom such as giving someone lifechanging advice for the slog of working your way through the backlog of Rites you'll probably find quite useful.<br /> <br /> Wisdom gets lost for things like wrongly assuming something is Wyrmish, losing or breaking a Talen or Fetish, being too 'modern', breaking the Litany, and being foolhardy.<br /> <hr /><br /> That's it for now. In future I'd like to cover Punishment Rites, how the advantages of your own circumstances can influence your Renown journey, and ultimately how it can be hard to build up Renown if you're solving problems created by the people deciding whether your actions were Honourable and Wise or not. I'd really welcome feedback! If there are any interesting examples of NPCs in the Rage Across books who are in weird situations with Renown, or any published adventures with really weird Renown rewards suggested to the ST, I'd love to hear about them, and anything in that vein.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-10T17:21:41-07:00","id":1549142,"title":"","content":"In my opinion the renown system has always been something that\u2019s more interesting if it\u2019s RPed out on some level, and I think that\u2019s a big part of what Moots are useful for both IC &amp; OOC. The Moot is the regularly occurring interval where Garou are all encouraged to gather and go through the ritualized recitation of their deeds and to receive recognition for having gained renown, as well as being the places where packs and war parties can form for the basis of performing deeds worthy of renown. Akin to what you mentioned, I think the Moot is a fantastic way of demonstrating the Garou way of politicking, with the elders sat nearest to the center and at the most liberty to direct matters and with the young turks sniping around the edges, attempting to whip up their own renown, or setting the stage for their plans. <br /> <br /> (Vancouver by Night isn\u2019t a perfect book but one of the reasons I always liked it was for the character of Gut-Tooth, the prophet of Gaia, on the werewolf side, who seemed to me like a great example of the kinds of antagonists who an ST can have thriving within the Nation itself)<br /> <br /> Something that I think is interesting to encourage with PC packs, who tend to start young and largely unestablished, is to have them be in a protectorate with a handful of septs and loners. This variety gives them the chance to triangulate with the sort of Garou most willing to tolerate their methods, however unorthodox, or alternatively shows them the benefits of being inside the tent pissing out. Ostensibly any Garou who learns the rites of recognition for permanent renown and raising rank, regardless of their standing with the predominant local leadership, can perform these rites. This creates an interesting possibility in my opinion where, the hypothetical imagined pack of rebel youths imagined by early editions, can seek out the means of advancement by sacrificing the benefits of the leading septs in exchange for aligning with the weirdos or spiritual patrons earned on quests.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11637-gryffon15","name":"Gryffon15","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11637_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"900"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-10T17:24:40-07:00","id":1549143,"title":"","content":"Nice deep dive.<br /> <br /> I'd add under the subjectivity of Renown, is that broadly the game presents Temporary Renown as something mostly biased towards the judgement of spirits, and Permanent Renown more in the hands of the Garou themselves. The spirits can reward you with dozens of temporary honor, but if no Garou will perform the Rite of Accomplishment for you, your Permanent Renown stagnates, your standing in the Nation doesn't go anywhere, and your Rank can't go up (which is also in the hands of other Garou rather than spirits). The game does leave the option of the spirits just acting on their own (this is mostly a dial to allow for different styles of play to be functional). This helps explain a lot of the dichotomies to Garou life without putting too much pressure on the ST to make it unfun to play. Since the ability to determine temporary Renown in-character basically doesn't exist, NPCs need to be convinced you've done enough things to warrant a Rite of Accomplishment, which is were so much bias can enter the equation. Does the NPC give you your Rite quickly because of your breeding, or demand more of you because you should be living up to your bloodline? Do they understand your Auspice duties to forgive a bit of less than perfect behavior or not care? Etc.<br /> <br /> This isn't a hard line of course, but as a broad framework it's a bit easier to understand how these dynamics manifest.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-10T19:48:29-07:00","id":1549145,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1549137-renown-rank-a-deep-dive#post1549137\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> So, all this to say, Renown isn't objective. There's a complex interplay of spiritual, legal, political, cultural and contextual factors at play. That is good and interesting, and helps to feed into themes of Personal Horror.</div> </div> </div> </div>There seems to be some kind of mix-up here. Werewolf is about splatter horror, not personal horror.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-11T03:15:17-07:00","id":1549148,"title":"","content":"Werewolf certainly has themes of personal horror, but you could just as easily say that all of those factors I describe feed into it being a grotesque satire, or punk, or a tragedy. Whatever theme you're going for in a WTA game, chances are it'll be well served by the vision of the Nation's corruption we're shown.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar617_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"617\" data-vbnamecard=\"617\">Heavy Arms</a> Absolutely! I'm planning to write a whole thing about how Rites come into it; we're told, for example, that if the Rite of Accomplishment fails then Garou will blame the recipient, not the person performing the rite, as they take it as an omen that the Garou wasn't worthy. While the Rite may fail simply because the Theurge got unlucky on the roll, or is bad at their job, or perhaps wasn't giving it their all even, it might also fail because all the Garou present THINK you have 10 points of temporary Renown, but in fact you don't. My overall POV is that Garou are at the core of Renown, and spirits are on the edge looking in, occasionally interfering, which actually pays the most respect to both narratively speaking.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11637-gryffon15\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar11637_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"11637\" data-vbnamecard=\"11637\">Gryffon15</a> I'm always very eager to encourage the ST to broaden up the setting to include a few local Septs. The most powerful might be the most corrupt, the friendliest might be a bit of a local 'joke' since it's city-based and has adapted to the modern world. There might be one full of secrets and opportunities for advancement, but which is run by insane extremists. It lets the ST have their cake and eat it, in a good way, since they can have cool likeable NPCs without making the setting feel tame or overly friendly. Yeah, the Master of the Rite may have a huge soft spot for the PCs and treat them like his nieces and nephews, but he's a Rank 4 Bone Gnawer in the region's weakest Sept, sitting in an Elder Council with only one true Elder.<br /> <br /> So if you wind up being a &quot;shoot first, ask questions later&quot; pack, you might be better off going to find the right audience for that. If you're a &quot;solve the root problem&quot; pack, well, there's probably a Sept in the area which is more ready to listen, even if it still has problems. That gives immersion and depth to the political landscape, and asking the party which Sept they want to visit each month for the Moot makes for an interesting decision. No matter which one you choose, there will ALWAYS be NPCs and opportunities you would have had at the others. If players go &quot;well, we can just show up to X Caern tomorrow and talk to Y, there's no rush&quot; I'd be quick to remind them that Garou are busy, they run off to the hills to do their jobs when Moots finish, and that the Moot is the only guaranteed time to see them. Plus, to be frank, showing up the day after the party to go &quot;hey, no you didn't see me last night, anyways I really need something from you&quot; is a bad look.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-11T11:00:33-07:00","id":1549159,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-size:16px\"><u><b>Rites and Renown</b></u></span><br /> <br /> Regardless of whether an individual Garou is gaining and losing Renown due to the influence of Garou or Spirits, or in many cases a combination, the process of gaining permanent 'dots' of Renown requires the input of the Garou's 'elders'. This is where Rites of Accomplishment come in. Meanwhile a Garou's Renown can be reduced, rightly or wrongly, through the use of Punishment Rites.<br /> <br /> W20 does establish that in unusual circumstances, when a Garou's accomplishments have no doubt whatsoever and their elevation in Glory, Honour or Wisdom should go without saying, that a permanent increase can happen without any Rite or outside interference.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> In very rare cases, the character\u2019s courage, wisdom, or honor is so exceptional that there is little doubt that the werewolf has increased in standing. If that is the case, you can simply decide to award a point of permanent Renown without the Rite or the challenge. This should happen very infrequently, reserved for cases where the tales of the character\u2019s deeds will last for a long time. </div> </div> </div>Failing this, Rites of Renown are required.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\"><u><b>Rites of Renown</b></u></span><br /> <br /> These Rites are performed by rolling Charisma + Rituals, so if you have a choice of which NPC handles your elevation, pick someone likeable or persuasive.<br /> <br /> <u>Rite of Boasting:</u> a great example of a Rite which allows Garou to 'game' the system of Renown somewhat. You can boast for yourself, or your whole pack, that you will achieve something boastworthy. If you do it you get bonus Glory, if you fail to do it you'll lose Glory. The potential bonus or loss is equal to half the number of successes, so with 10 successes you could stand to gain an additional 5 Glory, or lose 5 Glory. Naturally the bonus can't exceed the total for what you're boasting about doing. If taking out a certain threat nets you 3 Glory, the absolute maximum you could get for it with Boasting would be 6.<br /> <br /> Here we immediately see a place where discrepancies in culture, personality, and what role you are expected to fulfil within your society begin to creep in. A Bone Gnawer Metis with a withered leg bragging that he will claim the head of the Spiral Dancer due to be hunted that night will realistically be met with a level of heckling, disbelief and sabotage which won't occur when a Homid or Lupus of a more 'respectable' Tribe makes the boast. From what we're shown of the Garou we can infer that such a boast, from the Metis, would likely result in someone else going out of their way to secure the kill and claim the head themselves, so as to better 'instruct' their lessers on the importance of not making a boast they can't fulfil. A popular individual who has the right social 'mandate' to make such a boast, like a boisterous Lupus Cliath of the Get, may find that people who like them and are charmed by the idea of them succeeding do what they can to facilitate it, within reason.<br /> <br /> When you get into the weeds of this, you start to find that it isn't just that Might Makes Right, by the same measure <i>Right Makes Might</i>. Much of an Ahroun's power, most especially, comes from how swiftly they can accumulate Glory. If your ability to accumulate Glory is influenced by the degree to which your society sees you as a 'good fit' for it, then things like disability, class, Tribal affiliation, family importance, ancestry, Pure Breed, attractiveness, hell, neurotype, will begin to play a disproportionate role. I'm a tall and assertive guy, and to me the idea of bragging about how I'm totally going to do X and Y and Z is absolutely mortifying, so a level of passive discrimination is immediately at play. Of course such boasting is just as likely to backfire as it is to work, but when it does work, you're then slotted in at Fostern or Adren, at which point you are expected to handle more dangerous missions and hunt for more powerful foes, with the assistance of more underlings and access to more powerful tools; again, Right makes Might.<br /> <br /> <u>Rite of Wounding:</u> mostly this Rite serves as a means by which Cubs, and more rarely Cliaths, get some of the temporary Glory they require. The only especially noteworthy thing about this Rite from a sociological perspective is that some Tribes and Septs ensure the wound scars by rubbing ash into it. Part of the culture of the Garou is a focus on having survived battles, and bearing marks of those battles. You could see this as a kind of Machismo, an interest in being a warrior rather than a looker. It should go without saying that a Garou whose Homid form has distinctive scars now has a disadvantage in certain situations. They are more recognisable, easier to describe, and a random hunter is more likely to think &quot;huh, he looks the type&quot;. Of course there are also marginal benefits, like potentially looking more intimidating to someone who has never been in a fight! We can only assume that Garou who choose to scar their underlings, or who ask to be scarred themselves, know this and consider the warrior culture to be more important than marginal losses in strategy.<br /> <br /> <u>Rite of Accomplishment:</u> this Gift turns 10 temporary Glory, Honour or Renown into a single permanent point of the relevant Renown type. Immediately this serves an important function in pacing a Garou's ascent. No matter how glorious your deeds in a given month, short of some legendary accomplishment for your current Rank which enters into local myth immediately, you will at most gain 1 Glory, 1 Honour and 1 Wisdom with each passing month. And that, to be fair, would be an exceptionally fast accumulation of Renown! NPCs pass years and even decades without increasing Rank, so we can only assume that there is a normal amount of waxing and waning at work, otherwise the distribution of the Garou population in terms of Rank would be very top-heavy.<br /> <br /> Accessing this Rite is not a given. If someone refuses to perform it, and has enough clout to make it unlikely that others will perform it, you might find yourself running around a Moot trying to do favours or make promises. Push comes to shove you may have to Challenge someone to access this Rite, which could be very difficult if you are Rank 1 and there aren't any Rank 2 Garou who know the Rite in the Sept. Given that it goes against Garou Tradition for you to challenge a Rank 3 Adren, a Cliath in that position might just have to accept that they are powerless and need to attend a different Moot next time.<br /> <br /> This Rite, of course, can fail like any other. A Charisma 1 Rituals 2 Theurge (silly thought experiment, but feasible given that this is a Level 2 Ritual!) has a 87% chance of succeeding, or 74% if someone heckles the proceedings to try and stop you from receiving the Renown. With a more reasonable dice pool of 6 the odds are 96% and 88%. So, assuming that the Rite is rolled procedurally, there may be a 4% chance that you won't gain the Renown you're owed, or a 12% chance if someone heckles you. What's important to note is that if the Rite fails then you, the recipient, are generally blamed. Garou superstition holds that there must be some hidden fault in you. A Botch is worse; you are expected to undergo some kind of contrition before you can have <i>any</i> Rite of Accomplishment performed, even for other Renown types, due to a grave inner failing.<br /> <br /> What's important to note about this Rite is that ease of access is everything. A Silver Fang whose mother is the Master of the Rite, and who has all the right Backgrounds and Merits, will likely have an easier time accessing this Rite at a Moot, and may well have it performed by someone more competent. While every Rite demands silence and attention, there may well be more fanfare and a greater sense of 'occasion'. While this isn't necessarily an advantage at most Moots, it's worth noting that the sooner you get your Renown, the sooner you can Challenge to improve your Rank. And the sooner you have an empty temporary Renown track, the sooner you can begin earning Renown again, and a Moot is a great place to do so with challenges and riddles and advice to give and conflicts to resolve, along with hunts and special Rites and so on. As I said, Right makes Might.<br /> <br /> <u>Rite of Praising:</u> with the Rite of Accomplishment in mind, it can be easy to wonder what happens when a Cliath's quick thinking and bravery are pivotal or important in saving a Caern from invasion. This Rite handles that situation quite well. You get your relevant Renowns bumped up using the Rite of Accomplishment, and you may well have even gained another permanent point of one or more Renown types at the moment you did this great deed if the Storyteller wills it, but you may well still 'waste' a large amount of temporary Glory or similar. And so, given that you've gone far above and beyond what would ever be expected for your Rank, you receive this Rite as a special congratulations. You're employee of the month, have some Talens or a Fetish, and a nice social dice pool boost for a few months.<br /> <br /> I find myself wondering what happens if <i>this</i> Rite fails. Perhaps the takeaway is that those who decided the Garou was worthy of special congratulations were mistaken, and there is some hidden truth which makes the praiseworthy act more pedestrian than they had realised.<br /> <hr /><br /> What does this ultimately mean? What is the functional influence of Rites of Accomplishment? You could think of it as a kind of pressure valve. Outside of exceptional circumstances, a Garou's ascent is moderated, guided, and paced by the Garou above them. The difference between a rising star who rockets up to Adren in record time and an exceptional Fostern who &quot;still has much to learn&quot; would be the whims of Elders. Given Tribal, Camp and familial conflicts, preferences based on Auspice and Breed, political considerations and even the timing of a new Garou's 'arrival' into a Sept, it seems absolutely inconceivable that Elders wouldn't have their thumb-claw on the scale as it were.<br /> <hr /><br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\"><u><b>Rites of Punishment</b></u></span><br /> <br /> We're told that these Rites are performed only when necessary, and when conventional punishments (and presumably the standard losses of Renown) don't do the trick. Charisma + Rituals, difficulty 7 typically, and a failure tends to be interpreted in Garou superstition as a sign that what they were trying to punish is not serious enough to warrant a Punishment Rite. It's also noteworthy that W20 tells us that at times the spirit world will cause an attempted Punishment Rite to automatically fail due to its injustice, automatically warranting an Honour loss on the part of those performing it.<br /> <br /> I get into some examples below, but I think it's really important that PCs are shown NPCs suffering these Rites, and encounter (directly and indirectly) characters who have suffered such punishments. You could have an Elder talk about how a certain punishment set them straight and is the reason they are the hero they are today, for example, or the PCs could come across Garou whose otherwise promising careers have been destroyed by cruel and unusual punishments. Hell, a great villain would be a Philodox who has become an unwitting pawn of the Wyrm as they become harsher and harsher towards those who are straying, coming up with convincing and powerful arguments for why small infractions must be taken seriously (gotta be tough on crime!). This would be a great side plot in a story which emphasizes injustice as a major theme, for example one where the players are dealing with a Pentex subsidiary which builds private prisons and then does everything it can to ensure they get filled.<br /> <br /> Note that there are Punishment Rites in W20 which I won't cover here as they aren't relevant to the discussion of Renown.<br /> <br /> <u>Rite of the Jackdaw:</u> this Punishment Rite has little to do with Renown and more to do with breaking a promise of secrecy... though that, of course, does tend to lose a Garou some Honour. This is a simple punishment which makes the target begin to blab all their personal details, like who they have a crush on or which Elder scared them so much as a kid they wet themselves.<br /> <br /> <u>Rite of Ostracism:</u> a punishment used for 'lesser' crimes, all present declare that they've never heard of the punished Garou, and they are temporarily banished from their pack, Sept and so on. Everyone agrees to ignore them, and people only discard the punishment if absolutely necessary. It's also noted that some will &quot;accidentally&quot; attack or harm the banished individual. Receiving this punishment costs you 1 Glory, 5 Honour and 1 Wisdom, which could do almost nothing (if you have some temporary to spare!) or be absolutely devastating and temporarily knock you down a Rank. Note that this punishment is for 'lesser' crimes and that it seems to be considered normal by the Garou Nation at large; we are given no indication that it's rare or odd or old fashioned.<br /> <br /> As a result, my storytelling tip is to have an Ostracised NPC in your game! Perhaps they're the only one who would be able to help the PCs, but given that they won't exist until the next full moon... your choices are to break the punishment and seek out this Garou who 'doesn't exist', or to sit tight and wait. Giving succour to a Garou in need who has been punished in this way might be a strong defining character beat for a new PC, as would callously ignoring them to stick to the rules. A Child of Gaia who will pour water down any parched throat... unless they <i>deserve</i> to be thirsty of course is very different from a Child of Gaia who will help others even at risk to their own status, and highlighting that difference early on could be very interesting.<br /> <br /> Pretty juvenile as a punishment, and ultimately unproductive as ostracising a person only serves to further alienate them. Banning this punishment in the name of recognising and taking responsibility for every single Garou who has not betrayed or left the Nation would be a step in the right direction, but presumably this Rite must have advocates or it wouldn't exist! Make sure to have NPCs who have values which align with these punishments and who see them as inherently right and proper, and who are deeply offended by the suggestion that this ancient tradition stops. You can't just have ambient badness in your Sept with no nameable NPCs who are in favour of it.<br /> <br /> <u>Stone of Scorn:</u> this one is where I really start to cringe. We are told, and should take seriously, that Garou sit around doing this for hours. Just talking shit about someone. Someone who has committed some serious crime of course, but presumably that's already cost them a hefty amount of Honour at the very least. We're told that this Rite &quot;often&quot; takes a whole night and that the tales told become more and more extreme as time goes on. Gaia is dying, corporations are growing fatter and fatter while poverty intensifies, Banes are reaching new record concentrations with each passing month, and we're told that Garou sit around for a whole night saying mean things about someone who broke an important rule.<br /> <br /> Patience for this needs to be extremely limited. A senior Philodox metes out punishment (demotion, a new chore, a whipping, an unpleasant solo quest) and the matter is closed. Instead we have a tradition where Garou delight in excessive and even childish cruelty.<br /> <br /> Oh and the target loses 8 Honour and 2 Wisdom, naturally. If you want to show the weakness of a particular Sept you could have some theft, sabotage or desecration take place while everyone is howling with laughter at this Rite, only for the Sept at large to blame the person who was being punished for the 'distraction'.<br /> <br /> <u>Voice of the Jackal:</u> a Garou who has shamed their whole Tribe or Sept is punished using this Rite, which gives them a shrill high-pitched voice like a jackal. Until the punishment is ended you subtract 2 dice from all social rolls. This costs you 2 Glory and 5 Honour. If the one who punished you won't end the punishment, performing a great duty for Gaia can reverse it.<br /> <br /> Meeting a NPC who has suffered this Rite might help to introduce the PCs to what the Garou Nation is like, or what a given Sept would consider a crime which fits this punishment.<br /> <br /> <u>The Hunt:</u> this Rite is used as a means of honourable execution when a Garou needs to be killed but their Honour remains intact. Garou who die in The Hunt are forgiven of all their crimes.<br /> <br /> It's worth noting that a society where being forgiven in death is important is very different to the one most of us live in. The idea of your honour mattering when you are no longer alive is alien to us, but it isn't so strange for the Garou who have Ancestor Spirits and so on. The fact that such Garou aren't sent to fight the Wyrm tells its own tale; this isn't a series of punishments crafted with a logical plan, it's more a collection of traditions which are so deep-rooted that they're (seemingly) impossible to change.<br /> <br /> <u>Rite of the Omega Wolf:</u> this is used when a Pack wishes to punish their Alpha for failure. It's very funny to me that this Rank includes spitting on the Alpha, or even pissing on them, but the writeup implies that at the end of the Rite they're still the Alpha! Naturally it isn't guaranteed that any one member of your Pack will have the guts to outright challenge you, but this Rite is funny to me because it suggests that you could be too cowardly to defeat your Alpha (even taking it in turns to Challenge them until they are worn down) but brave enough to grab them as a group, tie them up, and haze them, and then go on obeying them. My assumption would be that the Garou Nation's stance is &quot;if your Alpha is such a failure, you should be able to take them in a Challenge, no?&quot;<br /> <br /> The Rite is self-defeating, for what it's worth. The problem is poor leadership, so you give a -3 dice penalty to the Alpha's leadership, which will only make things worse.<br /> <br /> <u>Satire Rite:</u> used as a more serious version of the Stone of Scorn, this Rite is basically a blistering satirical work intended to shame you so terribly that you drop in Rank. Given that a whole huge gathering of Garou needs to play along with it and let you do it, this Rite is probably very, very rarely used. I would point out that it only makes sense that a Garou who needed to be reduced in Rank would get there through losing enough Glory, Honour or Wisdom that they automatically drop their Rank... but this is more immediate, so it serves a function.<br /> <br /> What adds insult to injury is that the song enters into collective memory. A great plot-thread for a Galliard PC would be them learning a song which originated from one of these Rites, which is a bit like a limerick or folk tale at this point. They could later meet the Ancestor Spirit of the Garou who was satirized and hear their side of the story. Alternatively the subject could still be alive, and have moved to a different Sept where they've regained their lost rank or perhaps exceeded it. Finding them could be hard given that they would need to change their Deed Name at the first opportunity. If you wanted to show the spitefulness of an Elder, you could have a Galliard's Challenge for gaining Rank be to find the Garou described in the song, which might lead to the satire reaching the Garou's new Sept for the first time. &quot;Ah, makes sense, he's sent you after me to sing the song. Spread it here if you wish, there's some excellent rhyme, just know that if you do I will hunt down that old bastard and kill him, or die trying.&quot;<br /> <hr /><br /> What can we learn from these Rites? Well, a lot of time and effort goes into them. They are quite excessive and passionate, and clearly driven by a creature filled with Rage. None of these Punishments are quiet, proportionate or somber. They're visceral, base, highly public. This serves an important function on a wider level. It isn't just a punishment for a wrongdoer, it's a warning to any who might emulate them.<br /> <br /> It's also important to note that there is no strict Garou legal code. The Litany is like the Ten Commandments, there are rules there, but we don't have a minimum and maximum sentence for a given crime which is defined precisely as when you blah blah and blah with strict terms to distinguish it from other similar crimes. So again nepotism will rear its ugly head. Who needs 'making an example of'? Who 'has already suffered enough - they already lost Renown, and now they've formally performed a Rite of Contrition'?. In a society which is explicitly described as unjust and gripped by various bigotries, it's easy to imagine that the demographics of who gets Punishment Rites (Metis, Bone Gnawers, members of marginal Camps, and so on) will differ from those who simply suffer the loss of Renown and move on. Who has people ready to cover for them? Who has a sage mentor ready to tell them the right apology and gift to offer up to make the whole thing blow over? Who gets wrongly egged on to stand up for themselves after a small error, causing it to spiral into a bigger deal than it should have been?<br /> <br /> Of course certain protections can backfire! Pure Breed means high expectations. Being from a powerful family, being surrounded by loyal Kinfolk, having success after success, may well mean there are people who want to take you down a peg. It might not even be you they're hoping to harm, but whoever you're seen as being an extension of. A Cliath (of any background) who finds themselves suffering a Punishment Rite might be part of a larger scheme they know nothing about, their misdeed becoming a proxy battle between different Garou who are fractured on ideological or ethnic lines, or by past quarrels.<br /> <br /> The overall picture we can take from these Rites is a society which is, at times, lacking perspective. Suffering seems to be key to Garou existence. Changing Ways tells us that beatings are a common punishment, and that when Garou sleep with one another execution will be on the table for discussion. For all the Garou talk about being Gaia's front-line warriors, they seem to act like they have soldiers to spare. Keeping a Garou totally ignored for a month. Wasting a whole night chatting shit about someone. Letting an honourable criminal die in a hunt rather than die in battle against the enemy.<br /> <br /> When we look at Renown Rites we also see a huge range of potential outcomes without ever being explicitly discriminatory. Prompt, competent and warm Rites of Accomplishment might see a Tribe's Renown rise very rapidly. Begrudging last-minute Rites may see your ascent slowed considerably. It would be easy to imagine that if this were occurring it would be pointed out, but keep in mind that the fastest possible way to lose Honour and ruin your career is to question this system. W20 explicitly tells us that complaining is a great way to get yourself in even more trouble. This is a society where questioning being born into a rigid caste system based on your horoscope has serious consequences - you'd better believe that pointing out systemic discrimination and corruption is risky at best and shooting yourself in the foot (or head) at worst.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-11T15:22:28-07:00","id":1549169,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1549137-renown-rank-a-deep-dive?p=1549148#post1549148\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">My overall POV is that Garou are at the core of Renown, and spirits are on the edge looking in, occasionally interfering, which actually pays the most respect to both narratively speaking.</div> </div> </div> </div>This POV doesn't really mesh with the books though, with makes a lot of analysis from this POV a bit suspect. While the book leaves some room for interpretation on why this is the case for different group's preferences, the default mechanics of the game are that Renown rewards are automatic. They don't require other Garou to witness your actions for your temporary Renown to change. &quot;The Renown system isn\u2019t based purely on politics or community \u2014 the acts of an individual werewolf are distinct and indelible, regardless of what the pack or the sept thinks. In Werewolf, actions have consequences, even private ones.&quot; (W20 core, p. 246). This can't really work if Garou are the core of Renown and the spirits only &quot;look in from the edge.&quot; Whether you go with (IME is the most common for being the easiest to handle) &quot;spirits do it,&quot; angle, or something more nebulously built into the spiritual nature of the Garou themselves (which is still something imparted on them by Gaia and Luna anyway), the Garou can't really be core to the process if it happens without them.<br /> <br /> Keep in mind how this then impacts things like a failed Rite of Accomplishment. In-character, the Garou have no &quot;sense Temporary Renown&quot; ability. It's impossible for them to tell the difference between &quot;the ritemaster rolled poorly,&quot; and, &quot;the petitioner overstated their deeds and hide their misdeeds giving them impression they were worthy of acknowledgement.&quot; The Elder-biases politics of the Garou are obviously going to assume a Garou failed to disclose a negative action that meant the Rite failed than the higher Ranked Garou just didn't do things correctly. Even in most human societies it's expected that people &quot;put their best foot forward,&quot; by focusing on their good traits and downplaying anything negative. Yes, it's not fair when you have 10 temp and the NPC rolls for shit, and that's a reflection of Garou society's inequities, but the psychology at play isn't as alien when you take in the default way temp Renown works in the game.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1549137-renown-rank-a-deep-dive?p=1549159#post1549159\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">...if someone heckles the proceedings to try and stop you from receiving the Renown.</div> </div> </div> </div>The heckling aspect of this Rite is really undersold by it's minor mechanics. The difficulty increase is minimal, and a flat number that completely ignores the skills of the heckler(s). You basically never see PCs do this because they're effectively blowing temporary Renown to accomplish nothing. And even for NPCs, why should an Elder Ragabash Shadow Lord orchestrate a heckling of an up-and-coming Silver Fang hero when the most likely result is the Elder and their allies losing Renown (and a Cliath Ahroun can just crassly berate the petitioner for the same impact)? As one of the few ways to slow the roll of Renown-to-Rank without dipping into the punishment Rites, it's exceptionally lackluster and really kills the political side of the game by narrowing the options for political embarrassment of your rivals.<br /> <br /> This also really undercuts the Galliards because this is one of the few spaces for them to have battles over the narrative of Garou history. You can sing all the tales you want during that part of a Moot (for paltry Renown rewards, a different problem), but you can get 10 successes deriding a member of the Sept as utter scum that should seriously be considered worth of banishment as a Ronin... and still they easily get a Rite of Accomplishment through and a Renown bump.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It's also noteworthy that W20 tells us that at times the spirit world will cause an attempted Punishment Rite to automatically fail due to its injustice, automatically warranting an Honour loss on the part of those performing it. </div> </div> </div>I think the POV you have around Renown is coloring this a bit. Yes, there's no guarantee the spirits will step in, but as they oversee and empower these rites, they can autofail any they consider &quot;truly unjust.&quot;<br /> <br /> This has an important impact of how these Rites get used. Philodoxes (who really need that Honor) are going to keep to the idea of formal punishments being more extreme measures. Being too capricious or abusive with them is a fast track to losing Renown, and unlike Accomplishment the fault is aided at the judge trying to lay down a wrongful verdict here. Even if higher Rank Garou want to be heavy handed with these Rites, they still need enough justification to warrant them or they put their power at risk.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Pretty juvenile as a punishment, and ultimately unproductive... </div> </div> </div>I think it's worth considering the massive differences living in a highly isolated religious fundamentalist society has on people; even more so when you add it the psychology of military life on top of it. But put a huge pressure on conformity and the &quot;greater good,&quot; because to a certain extent disunity is an existential threat to them (the communities, not necessarily the individuals), and it works because it taps into very primal instincts wolves and humans share as species that rely on communal activities to survive.<br /> <br /> It is better for a wolf pack to &quot;deal&quot; with a wolf that can't keep their shit together and keeps messing up hunts by spooking prey early. Soldiers that don't follow orders and doom their whole squads; who would very much rather be down a fighter than all be dead. A rural religious community that opts to live in a semi-subsistence level of agricultural lifestyle cannot afford to deal with a troublemaker's constant contrarian antics when there's a winter to endure every year. Etc.<br /> <br /> It might feel unproductive to most modern people living in developed countries where being shunned like this isn't an immediate threat to your livelihood, but if you look at specific communities or countries that aren't doing so well... these sorts of social tactics are very productive for keeping the social order. It's important to remember that regardless of how &quot;juvenile&quot; it seems, the more focused people are on day-to-day survival, the more immediate their thinking becomes broadly. They aren't thinking about what years of this sort of treatment might do to someone over time (and might consider further alienation an excuse to just be rid of someone that's a net negative on the community), because that time frame isn't part of their conditioning.<br /> <br /> This isn't really some sort of ancient tradition that needs to be protected... it's how most humans on Earth live: under the threat where sustained ostracism is effectively a death sentence, and temporary ostracism exists to foreshadow what happens if you don't get back in line. It's a simple matter of survival in a harsh world: the closer you are the death, the less you can tolerate screw ups in your community. &quot;More of us would be better,&quot; is not always true when you get to real situations. Having more Garou warriors to fight the Wyrm later doesn't matter if someone keeps undermining the Garou now and their enemies take advantage of it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Gaia is dying, corporations are growing fatter and fatter while poverty intensifies, Banes are reaching new record concentrations with each passing month, and we're told that Garou sit around for a whole night saying mean things about someone who broke an important rule. </div> </div> </div>Yes. Because the rule is that important. This isn't a defense of this sort of social conditioning, but I've interacted with enough communities that really do think this way and you can only really understand it when you consider the immediacy of their perspective I mentioned above.<br /> <br /> For such people they're not wasting time that could be used fighting the Wyrm, they're ensuring that the fight against the Wyrm is possible because without the rules they can't comprehend succeeding. Allowing rule breaking to go without major public comment in proportion to how bad the rule-breaking is considered, is the same as saying the rules don't matter at all. If you believe that a strong chain of command is vital to victory, then you have to seriously punish violations of the chain of command, or you breed weak links in the chain because it becomes questionable that it's all so vital. Hence a night of making an example out of someone is considered a good use of time. One night of enforcement is dozens of battles without insubordination.<br /> <br /> You talk about how alien honor-even-in-death is to many of us, but it's a real thing for many cultures (and used to be even more common). This is true here as well. It's not how you think, it's not something I consider a healthy way to think, but it's still a way plenty of people in the real world actually think; and have lived lives that confirm their biases on the matter to the extent where you can't &quot;logic&quot; them into seeing differently.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <u>Rite of the Omega Wolf:</u> this is used when a Pack wishes to punish their Alpha for failure. It's very funny to me that this Rank includes spitting on the Alpha, or even pissing on them, but the writeup implies that at the end of the Rite they're still the Alpha! </div> </div> </div>I think the Rite implies the opposite, &quot;If he ever becomes a pack alpha again, he suffers a \u20133 penalty to all Leadership actions until he either relinquishes the position or wins some great victory for his pack through his leadership.&quot; This implies he's not alpha after this is over, and even if he can take it back by winning challenges, he's in for a rough ride until he can redeem himself.<br /> <br /> Sure, it might seem &quot;cowardly&quot; since this would only happen if the back doesn't have anyone that can one-on-one the current alpha, but it's well established in the Litany's teachings that this sort of uprising against bad leadership is a valid remedy. I think, especially if you consider this to strip the target of their position (again the phrasing of the -3 penalty is on &quot;if&quot; they get alpha back), the Garou nation is fine leaving the shame on the alpha that did such a shit job in the first place.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-11T16:33:08-07:00","id":1549170,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1549137-renown-rank-a-deep-dive?p=1549169#post1549169\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> This POV doesn't really mesh with the books though, with makes a lot of analysis from this POV a bit suspect. While the book leaves some room for interpretation on why this is the case for different group's preferences, the default mechanics of the game are that Renown rewards are automatic.</div> </div> </div> </div>To be clear, I'm not saying that Temporary Renown adjustments aren't automatic - by default they are, and that's what I'm working off. That said, it's somewhat apparent that renown gains seem to vary depending on ambient politics and what are considered acceptable enemies. Like I say in my first post, there's a lot of different types of Garou who have different ideas of what constitutes a kill-on-sight enemy, what the rules are, and so on. So while your renown adjusts automatically, we're left to infer from the fact that there are Elders in weirdo extremist Septs that they aren't suffering horrible losses of Honour and Wisdom from their routine activities. Whether that's because the spirits vary (different Ancestors, different Tribal Totem, different Brood as an audience etc.) or because the Garou that you will be reporting to later, and who you are ultimately subordinate to vary isn't 100% clear.<br /> <br /> The context of me saying &quot;Garou are at the core of Renown&quot; is that access to permanent points in Renown is gatekept by Garou, almost always. I also do mention that the Rite of Accomplishment may fail because you're presumed to be at 10 temp Renown but you actually aren't! So, to be clear, I'm aware that Renown is handed out as the action unfolds, and that the fact that spirits (Ancestor Spirits feel like the neatest example) can see you at all times is important to Renown as a concept.<br /> <br /> Agreed that Heckling or trying to interrupt the ascension of others in Renown/Rank should be more of a focus. +2 difficulty on a difficulty 4 roll isn't much of anything.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I think the POV you have around Renown is coloring this a bit. Yes, there's no guarantee the spirits will step in, but as they oversee and empower these rites, they can autofail any they consider &quot;truly unjust.&quot; </div> </div> </div>You've basically just reworded what I'm saying - to be clear I'm neutrally relaying that if a Punishment Rite is sufficiently unjust then the spirits won't empower it and the Rite will automatically fail. I'm not implying that such rites are always unjust or anything like that, don't worry.<br /> <br /> As for the appropriateness of Punishment Rites, it's important to keep in mind that while Garou live in a harsh world, and many die young, part of the role of the Elder is to take a longer view. Yeah, if you're living life on the edge and total obedience to the chain of command is absolutely critical, it's great to have good ways to incentivise obedience. Like, for example, a Renown system! A system where doing the wrong thing loses you renown. Then there are regular punishments, ways of punishing people without invoking the powers of the Spirit world, which gives you more flexibility and less escalation. There are also ways of seeking solutions to the underlying problem and conflict, and to be fair, the Renown system does award Wisdom and Honour for doing such things.<br /> <br /> The issue is that we're told that the Rite of Ostracism is used for 'lesser' crimes, with no example given. It immediately invites the reader to wonder what is meant by minor, and what is meant by crime. You are of course dealing with hyper-violent hormonal Rage-fueled warriors who can easily fly off the handle, and it makes sense that such extreme creatures would need some extreme punishments in their arsenal. I'm fully aware, for what it's worth, that there are a lot of things which make the Garou feel like this approach is necessary. It isn't just tradition, it's desperation, anger, something akin to religious fundamentalism, and a real need for everyone to stay on target.<br /> <br /> But when you get to the Stone of Scorn and Satire Rite, you're basically punishing someone so severely, in such a public and humiliating way, that that person is now tainted. Imagine your entire community, not just your whole family but the extended family, and every family they're friends with, and every family friend of those families, and all of your heroes and celebrities in the region, everyone you've ever looked up to or wanted to emulate, spending the night coming up with worse and worse things to say about you like it's Whose Litany Breach Is It Anyway. The idea that such punishment could ever make a person better is just... mad. And I'm highlighting that not because it's poor writing. I think it's excellent writing. But I also think that it's worth pointing out that the Garou Nation is repeatedly shooting itself in the foot by creating future Haranos and suicides, future betrayals, future bitter failures who weigh down their pack, future abusive parents, and so on.<br /> <br /> I think it's very fitting to the Garou that punishments like the Stone of Scorn exist. My overall take is that it's very important that we recognize that it's counterproductive whilst also recognizing why the Garou think it's productive. That future suicide? Always knew he didn't have what it took. Future betrayal? Always knew he was a bad egg. He's a bad dad? That can't be because we gave him PTSD from a harrowing night of abuse which haunts him to this day... no, he always would've been a bad dad, and his kids are bound to be like him too. It's very, very easy to understand why the Garou think this is the right way, but it's also important to call it what it is.<br /> <br /> That's why I found it very refreshing when a Metis in Changing Ways used the words 'child abuse' to describe his upbringing. There's no need for a follow-up going &quot;well the Garou think that the Metis are hated by Gaia, so it actually makes sense that they would-&quot; since we know all that. I don't think WTA would be a better setting if you got rid of this stuff. It would be like getting rid of Primogen being corrupt or Sidhe being elitist. Me calling the punishment rites immature, which they are, doesn't mean I don't understand why they are that way, just to be clear!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-11T19:35:02-07:00","id":1549180,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1549137-renown-rank-a-deep-dive#post1549137\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There is, however, also the matter of incomplete information. W20 establishes that the spirit world will witness your acts even if other Garou are unaware of them. If you break the Litany, for example, you will lose Renown even if other Garou never find out. If you pretend to have dishonoured yourself for honourable reasons, such as pretending a Metis cub is yours in order to protect Gaia in some fashion, the Storyteller may well rule that the Honour on your sheet winds up being better than what your fellows believe of you. Whether a Garou framed of some crime loses Renown or not is also a nebulous area, but given that loss of Rank and Renown can be forced by Punishment Rites and the actions of others, it's clear that framing can have tangible results for the dots on your sheet.</div> </div> </div> </div>While it make sense that some spirits might be some observing things, I think it is ridiculous to consider Garou are being constantly observed in secret so that Renown is somehow objective. It actually does not make sense in character, and it provides for bad stories.<br /> <br /> However, I do think there is a need by many players to have Renown be objective. Many players want &quot;fairness&quot; in Renown in the same way STs award XP. This is driven by OOC considerations especially in large multiplayer games in LARP and online. Since Renown drives Rank, and Rank is how you can buy more powerful Gifts, players can easily get upset if they think they are being cheated.<br /> <br /> As an in character element, there's no problem if the Bone Gnawer characters are being denied their proper Renown in favor of less unworthy Silver Fangs. That can make for a good setting or story. As an in character element, there's no problem if one character is framing another Garou for a crime they committed. But out of character, screwing over players is a bad idea. <br /> <br /> In smaller tabletop games, an ST can use these elements to show how NPCs are treated, while still arranging things so that they don't impact PCs themselves. In large online games or LARPs, the player characters are often most of the chronicle characters so you adopt a more objective standard.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-11T19:36:15-07:00","id":1549181,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1549137-renown-rank-a-deep-dive?p=1549170#post1549170\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That said, it's somewhat apparent that renown gains seem to vary depending on ambient politics and what are considered acceptable enemies.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm... not really sure what passage you feel supports this so strongly. While yes, there is some subjectivity in temp Renown awards, they're not phrased to support this being broadly the case; rather exceptions. There's nothing suggesting that how much Honor you lose for lying varies from Sept to Sept or Tribe to Tribe. There's certainly situational variations, but that's not the same thing.<br /> <br /> What Renown award suggests that &quot;acceptable enemies&quot; is a variable? Yes, the ST can always add that in, but the rewards don't say &quot;Get receive +1 Glory when defeating fae threats&quot; despite there being such riders for things like not losing Renown if shit talking a Gnawer. The book only separates Wyrm and non-Wyrm threats as categories. Where is this so &quot;apparent?&quot;<br /> <br /> Politics and such might enter in with things like, &quot;we have an alliance with the local mages, why did you kill one?&quot; because that would mean they weren't threats and killing them wasn't a glorious deed (and probably a notorious deed instead).<br /> <br /> As for extremists... I think you're massively overestimating the Renown system's constraints on them. Without higher Ranking werewolves around to use Punishment rites on extremists to keep their approaches suppressed... the Renown awards alone aren't really going to do much to stop them. The vast majority of Garou extremists camps are either extremists that don't directly work against Renown (which is an important narrative element of its own) or do have problems with maintaining status (the two cannibal groups being examples there). Then you have things in the setting like the Red Talons having a rite (I don't believe it made it into W20) specifically so other Tribes couldn't hit them with Punishment rites that would otherwise let the other Tribes diminish Red Talon control over some of the most valuable Caerns in the world via vastly increasing the Renown loss that they're otherwise skirting around.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The context of me saying &quot;Garou are at the core of Renown&quot; is that access to permanent points in Renown is gatekept by Garou, almost always. </div> </div> </div>This doesn't really help me understand what you're trying to say. I had been saying that temporary and permanent Renown should be viewed a bit differently regarding spirits vs. Garou as who controls advancement of them more... and you responded that the Garou are the core to Renown, which you are clarify only means permanent Renown so... that really just confuses me.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You've basically just reworded what I'm saying - to be clear I'm neutrally relaying... </div> </div> </div>I didn't find your initial phrasing as neutral as I think you intended it to be; esp. regarding the role of spirits broadly in Renown. The spirits don't just swoop in a save Garou from unjust punishments. Punishment rites go &quot;through&quot; spirits to work, and the spirits get to veto them in process (something largely not true of Rites). They are part of the process, not lurkers, while your initial phrasing kept the language of spirits being tangential to the process instead of a key component to it.<br /> <br /> And you haven't really responded to the psychological impact on Garou dishing out punishment Rites that the spirits are making sure that they're staying in some level of fair boundaries.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The issue is that we're told that the Rite of Ostracism is used for 'lesser' crimes, with no example given. </div> </div> </div>We're also told that punishment rites aren't brought into play until other methods are exhausted first as well. The Rite of Ostracism, as the books portray things, is not something for first time offenders. It's for Garou that have repeatedly failed to internalize lessons via other methods.<br /> <br /> Sure, the books are vague on &quot;minor&quot; (&quot;crime&quot; feels nitpicky to go after for not having a precise definition in the book), but that isn't inherently a problem if the general idea that punishment rites are already exceptional tools to have to use as presented at the start of their sub-section.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But when you get to the Stone of Scorn and Satire Rite, you're basically punishing someone so severely, in such a public and humiliating way, that that person is now tainted. </div> </div> </div>I think this ignores that the Garou do have a strong culture of spiritual redemption (even if sometimes suicidal in its nature). As you noted, they care enough about honor to value dying with their honor intact rather than living in permanent infamy. A Garou that endures and reclaims or even surpasses their previous standing will end up a positive figure in Garou legends for it. As you noted, it's good to have NPCs that have endured punishments to help so that this is an accepted part of Garou culture and show how they rationalize this system. An Elder that has their Rank despite being subjected to the Satire Rite earlier in life is a formidable character to introduce to a story. The fact that many Garou are likely to mentally break instead of endure is kinda the point.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The idea that such punishment could ever make a person better is just... mad. </div> </div> </div>It's not about making them better. It's about making them comply, and making everyone else complicit.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But I also think that it's worth pointing out that the Garou Nation is repeatedly shooting itself in the foot by creating future Haranos and suicides, future betrayals, future bitter failures who weigh down their pack, future abusive parents, and so on. </div> </div> </div>Except we see this sorts of psychological tactics in the real world, and the only thing on your list that's exceptional common in them is future abusive parents... who perpetuate the culture that created them (not something the Garou are going to find a negative).<br /> <br /> I'm going to get into a more specific real world example: Scientology. If there's any Scientologists on here, sorry, but you're part of a controversial group. Scientology's expertise in these sorts of tactics are why many countries consider them a cult instead of recognizing them as a proper religion with the attendant rights of religious freedoms. Look up what Scientology means with phrases like, &quot;disconnection,&quot; &quot;fair game,&quot; or &quot;auditing.&quot; You might see these things as &quot;immature&quot; but they are anything but. And all the more scary for how downright effective they really are.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It's very, very easy to understand why the Garou think this is the right way, but it's also important to call it what it is. </div> </div> </div>All the more reason not to dismiss it with words like juvenile or immature. These are deadly tools of social control in the hands of people that are willing to abuse them; refined over thousands of years of humans attempting to control other humans. Because these are very matured tactics that are used because they are very effective; and are unfortunately not as counterproductive as those of us that find them morally reprehensible would like them to be.<br /> <br /> My concern isn't something like, &quot;Oh no, Twiggins wants to destroy the game by cutting it's core satirical criticism,&quot; or some such. I'm concerned that you're really not showing an understanding of how these tools work in reality. How they're scary and worth using WtA to explore because they're not childish things that are easily defeated by adult minded people. I'm not talking about the books a lot for a reason. I'm focusing on real world stuff for a reason. That reason is that this shit <i>works</i> on people.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-11T20:09:27-07:00","id":1549182,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1549137-renown-rank-a-deep-dive?p=1549159#post1549159\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's also important to note that there is no strict Garou legal code. The Litany is like the Ten Commandments, there are rules there, but we don't have a minimum and maximum sentence for a given crime which is defined precisely as when you blah blah and blah with strict terms to distinguish it from other similar crimes.</div> </div> </div> </div>This actually is not true from an in character perspective, though it is from an out of character perspective. The Litany, in fact, is a legal code that would take many hours (perhaps even days) to recite in its &quot;long form&quot;. It is much more simple than the legal codes of today's bureaucratic states, but probably not much different from the codes memorized by Norse lawspeakers. Of course the Litany isn't just one code. Every tribe and every sept has its own variant which may in turn be modified by decisions made by Great Moots, local Concolations, and Imperial moots. I imagine the &quot;standard&quot; Litany is based on what was decided at the great Concord that ended the Impergium.<br /> <br /> We know the Litany by its thirteen main precepts, but every corebook makes it clear there is a much longer code. Of course, that's because as players that is usually all we need to run the game. STs can decide on things on the fly. We don't need a detailed legal code to run the game 99.99% of the time. That's why so many people forget the Litany is not like the Traditions in Vampire, but a long longer code whose 13 precepts are summaries. But in character, that legal code definitely exists although all its details have never been written down. For good reason, I doubt few players would buy such a dry sourcebook. If PCs are looking for some kind of loophole, saving grace, winning point, or other things we associate with legal dramas, the ST can make up something on the fly and actually tell the PC to roll their Law ability trait.<br /> <br /> Of course, sometimes it can be hard for STs to figure out what kind of crimes should be punished by what Rite. Should action X be punished by mere loss of Renown, the Rite of Ostracism, the Stone of Scorn, or something else? What capital crimes are executed via The Great Hunt versus Gaia's Vengeful Teeth versus the ultra-rare (because it is only mentioned in Werewolf Players Guide 1e) Rite of the Shattered Soul?<br /> <br /> Because I'm the kind of person who thinks too much, and more importantly wanted a list of Garou NPCs who commit crimes so players who were a Philodox could actually have something to do in game (and earn Renown for their auspice role!), I once started a thread about that. In case you find it useful for this thread, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1391585-crime-examples-for-the-rites-of-punishment\">here is a link</a>.<br /> <br /> This came about out of frustration in an online game where there was essentially nothing for Philodoxes to do. PCs in general don't violate the Litany so Philodoxes can't render judgment and thus earn Honor Renown much slower than other auspices earn Wisdom or Glory. So the solution is to have some NPCs commit certain crimes (or to be fair, disreputable behaviors that aren't technically crimes, but still punished in some way) at some point. Make up a short little list, assign certain negative personality traits to some NPCs, and I now have some subplots I can run that can help PCs earn Honor.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-11T23:31:56-07:00","id":1549183,"title":"","content":"It would have been nice if, at some point over WtA's history, they'd actually done some examples of local Litanies to help elaborate on that point. I get that books have other priorities, but the Rage Across the [Insert Area] books would have likely been improved by some pages dedicated towards what the local Litany/Litanies look like in more detail. It's great for the whole game line to see that in practice, and great for helping the authors do some local world building by doing the thought experiments of what has shaped local Garou laws in specific directions and precedents. It's certainly not a topic the various ST books ever really gave the love it needed to help STs craft it themselves either; so some examples would have at least helped.<br /> <br /> It's also good for the Galliards because local laws come from local history. A player that struggles with coming up with tales to sing beyond the pack's exploits or similar prompts can have a slew of prompts in weird local laws and how they came about. A good reason not to get too detailed unless it's important so the players can add their own creative spin on things.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-12T02:27:49-07:00","id":1549184,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1549137-renown-rank-a-deep-dive?p=1549182#post1549182\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> This actually is not true from an in character perspective, though it is from an out of character perspective. The Litany, in fact, is a legal code that would take many hours (perhaps even days) to recite in its &quot;long form&quot;. It is much more simple than the legal codes of today's bureaucratic states, but probably not much different from the codes memorized by Norse lawspeakers. Of course the Litany isn't just one code. Every tribe and every sept has its own variant which may in turn be modified by decisions made by Great Moots, local Concolations, and Imperial moots. I imagine the &quot;standard&quot; Litany is based on what was decided at the great Concord that ended the Impergium.<br /> <br /> We know the Litany by its thirteen main precepts, but every corebook makes it clear there is a much longer code. Of course, that's because as players that is usually all we need to run the game. STs can decide on things on the fly. We don't need a detailed legal code to run the game 99.99% of the time. That's why so many people forget the Litany is not like the Traditions in Vampire, but a long longer code whose 13 precepts are summaries. But in character, that legal code definitely exists although all its details have never been written down. For good reason, I doubt few players would buy such a dry sourcebook. If PCs are looking for some kind of loophole, saving grace, winning point, or other things we associate with legal dramas, the ST can make up something on the fly and actually tell the PC to roll their Law ability trait.<br /> <br /> Of course, sometimes it can be hard for STs to figure out what kind of crimes should be punished by what Rite. Should action X be punished by mere loss of Renown, the Rite of Ostracism, the Stone of Scorn, or something else? What capital crimes are executed via The Great Hunt versus Gaia's Vengeful Teeth versus the ultra-rare (because it is only mentioned in Werewolf Players Guide 1e) Rite of the Shattered Soul?<br /> <br /> Because I'm the kind of person who thinks too much, and more importantly wanted a list of Garou NPCs who commit crimes so players who were a Philodox could actually have something to do in game (and earn Renown for their auspice role!), I once started a thread about that. In case you find it useful for this thread, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1391585-crime-examples-for-the-rites-of-punishment\">here is a link</a>.<br /> <br /> This came about out of frustration in an online game where there was essentially nothing for Philodoxes to do. PCs in general don't violate the Litany so Philodoxes can't render judgment and thus earn Honor Renown much slower than other auspices earn Wisdom or Glory. So the solution is to have some NPCs commit certain crimes (or to be fair, disreputable behaviors that aren't technically crimes, but still punished in some way) at some point. Make up a short little list, assign certain negative personality traits to some NPCs, and I now have some subplots I can run that can help PCs earn Honor.</div> </div> </div> </div>Thanks, that's actually really useful to know! And I agree that lawkeeping is a role with very limited role for a PC in many cases. Unless a PC is wrongly accused of a crime, in a typical pack game, you won't have as much to do as you'd hope. There's more to Philodox than being a judge or lawyer of course, but having NPCs who need judgement, punishment, correction, help to stay on track, or who end up getting worse and then need worse punishment, seems like a great role for the Philodox to play a part in.<br /> <br /> In fact I could see a Rank 2 or 3 Garou who has messed up having a Rank 1 Philodox be involved in choosing their punishment. Partly because it involves the PC in drama which would normally go over their heads, but also because that's sorta part of the punishment, in a muted and understated way. Obviously any Philodox has their honour and is worthy of respect, and no one polite will go &quot;this pup is choosing my punishment?!&quot;, but it's an unspoken part of the arrangement. Discussing the nature of the crime, hearing the opinions of two different Philodox on what kinds of punishments they've used in the past, perhaps hearing a plea (or the opposite) from the Garou's alpha or family member, or some last second change to the evidence when you're about to proclaim judgement, could all be interesting. The situation should be low stakes enough, at least in the beginning, that it's believable that it would be considered a good &quot;training wheels&quot; (again, not language anyone would explicitly use) case for a Rank 1-2 Philodox.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-12T02:51:11-07:00","id":1549185,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar617_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"617\" data-vbnamecard=\"617\">Heavy Arms</a> I'm going off the fact that the word 'threat' inherently comes with a POV. A random Sidhe kid who is bossing other kids on a playground around isn't a threat... unless you and yours consider any and all Sidhe to be a threat, given that they are the same elves who enslaved your ancestors even if they pretend to forget. The naive (or blasphemous?) shamans occupying a random node they've cultivated aren't a threat, unless you consider any and all humans draining Gaia's lifeblood to be threats, tics draining her precious blood who must be eliminated. A family of rival Fianna kinfolk aren't a threat, unless you're a mother's fundamentalist who genuinely sees them as a roadblock on the path to saving Gaia. A den of Bastet or Gurahl cubs aren't a threat... except that, to many Garou during the War of Rage, they were. Hell, say you're carefully cleaning up after a battle against BSDs and you stumble across hidden cubs. Are they a threat? I'm sure the answer seems really obvious to most Werewolf STs, everyone knows the answer, no discussion needed, but if you were to poll a bunch of them you'd get different answers, and those different answers relate to different values, or even more likely relate to different perceived values of the setting.<br /> <br /> If Garou performing such acts, say slaughtering a bunch of Sidhe who were no threat to Gaia, or killing a bunch of dens full of Bastet cubs in the War of Rage, weren't gaining the temporary Glory they expect to be gaining, then at the next Moot they'd have the Rite of Accomplishment fail. And if that became a pattern across Garou society across many generations, that Garou who destroy these 'threats' don't get their flowers, then the various Auspices would likely start reading into it. Now naturally things wouldn't always be so cut and dry, in the same month you may well fight a bunch of Banes! But that's the take-home message. While I could understand if someone came up with a different interpretation of what we're presented with, to me it's hard to imagine a world where the Garou didn't get plenty of Glory from all their worst mistakes. I think it feeds very well into the tragedy and horror of the setting if the careers of many great and mighty Elders has their careers built on fat piles of Glory (and to a lesser extent Honour and Wisdom) gained from terrible mistakes that the Garou Nation is paying for to this day.<br /> <br /> For a more common example, away from history and extremists, you could think of workers on a devastating construction. Are the mundane workers 'threats'? I think the result will depend on the table, and the adventure. There will have been times in a single Chronicle where the answer would be yes, then later no, perhaps as a ST or the table evolve over the years. What counts as a threat could potentially say something about the ST's understanding or perspective of the politics and expectations of the Garou in the story, or the assumptions made in writing the story, or less likely something about their own politics and beliefs. If you're trying to figure out why this is the case from an in-universe perspective, the easiest explanation would be that the spirits differ (different ancestors, different broods as audiences) or that the Garou differ (Garou follow their leaders, and the Renown you're receiving reflects the values of your Elders by default).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-12T03:01:50-07:00","id":1549186,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1549137-renown-rank-a-deep-dive?p=1549180#post1549180\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> While it make sense that some spirits might be some observing things, I think it is ridiculous to consider Garou are being constantly observed in secret so that Renown is somehow objective. It actually does not make sense in character, and it provides for bad stories.<br /> <br /> However, I do think there is a need by many players to have Renown be objective. Many players want &quot;fairness&quot; in Renown in the same way STs award XP. This is driven by OOC considerations especially in large multiplayer games in LARP and online. Since Renown drives Rank, and Rank is how you can buy more powerful Gifts, players can easily get upset if they think they are being cheated.<br /> <br /> As an in character element, there's no problem if the Bone Gnawer characters are being denied their proper Renown in favor of less unworthy Silver Fangs. That can make for a good setting or story. As an in character element, there's no problem if one character is framing another Garou for a crime they committed. But out of character, screwing over players is a bad idea.<br /> <br /> In smaller tabletop games, an ST can use these elements to show how NPCs are treated, while still arranging things so that they don't impact PCs themselves. In large online games or LARPs, the player characters are often most of the chronicle characters so you adopt a more objective standard.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah nodding along with this. I think that the idea of Garou being observed by spirits at all times kiiiiiiiiiinda works, if it's explained in greater depth and the writers give a clear answer, instead of going &quot;maybe this, maybe that&quot;. If the main role of certain Ancestors is just to observe Garou of their Tribe and reward/punish their behaviour, that could make sense. It might even make it a potential plot point that you think you could find a killer by traveling to your Ancestral Homeland in the Umbra and questioning your Ancestors.<br /> <br /> But it leaves me feeling like... if the spirit world is so omniscient, despite the thick Gauntlet which is getting thicker as years go by, why aren't they sharing all that intel? They have every reason to. As an explanation it feels a bit hollow, unless it were very carefully considered and written by a writer and/or game designer better than me by far.<br /> <br /> And yeah, the issue of a fair game in an unfair setting is always really important. Bone Gnawers getting mistreated by and large shouldn't make it so that your BG PC lags behind the others in Rank. Hitting that balance is very, very hard! I'm reminded of how in Skyrim racism is a big 'theme' (though using that word feels like it's giving the writers too much credit), but your character is basically racially agnostic. No one cares whether you're a Nord or a Dunmer or an Argonian, including the guy who spends his days shouting slurs at Dunmer or the people who keep Dunmer stuck in a little ghetto. The disconnect between narrative and mechanics is so sharp that it feels like your character is a visitor from another world, or that they have some special pass stuck to their armour. There's definitely a balance that can be struck.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-12T10:00:53-07:00","id":1549190,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-size:14px\"><u><b>Gaming the System</b></u></span><br /> <br /> Alright, so lying about your accomplishments absolutely doesn't work. Telling great tales bulks out your Renown a bit, but that's a feature, not a bug. Timing your big wins? Well if you can organize that, sure, but you really shouldn't leave a pack of Fomori until your 'career schedule' lets you tackle it. Even if the risk of that being figured out is very, very slim it would look bad if anyone did a deep dive into your calendar, which feels like a very Glass Walker thing to analyze. Bragging ahead of time helps, but again, that's intended, and it does introduce the risk of another Pack interfering to take you down a peg or kill-steal.<br /> <br /> The big thing that comes to mind, setting wise, is simple privilege. Say you have the pooled Kinfolk, Resources, Armory, Mentor, Contacts, Allies and so on that make things just... easier. You see, the crux of Renown is your accomplishments, especially for Glory. Taking out a 'minor' Wyrm threat, whatever minor counts as for your Rank, is worth the same Renown whether it was a huge struggle against adversity, hunting night and day for leads on a hard-to-find foe, or whether the whole thing was handed to you, target-wise, on a silver platter. That's where Backgrounds and Merits come in.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Glory</b></u><br /> <br /> Every single member of your pack gaining a permanent level of Glory every single month is achievable, with the right organization and forward planning. With a well-developed information network (ideally in both worlds), the kind a Garou with extensive Kinfolk and Contacts might have, you could find yourself with more options of different things to hunt. A healthy mix of Banes, Fomori, Leeches and so on makes it look less like a Renown farm and more like you are proactively keeping an area clean and under control. Such a 'proactive' style does come with its risks, you're poking your nose into conspiracies and plans which are likely beyond your ability to fully vet, but that's where Resources and other Backgrounds are a big deal.<br /> <br /> Extensive spying, bribery, leveraging of allies and contacts, kitting out your squad with body armour and various useful weapons (to be Dedicated as Talismans via the Rite ASAP of course) can turn a fraught and potentially deadly combat into a walk in the park. Of course Garou are living weapons themselves, but tear gas can achieve in one turn what a single PC might struggle to get done in several rounds, given how turn economy works out with large groups of enemies.<br /> <br /> What we're looking at here, basically, is the difference between PCs and NPCs, in the simplest terms. But it's also the difference between packs who were born with silver spoons in their mouths and packs thrown together with awkward leftovers. So, in the medium of storytelling, you could show how at a Moot you have a pack of down-on-their-luck Garou who, in objectively a very impressive way, managed to make a lot out of a little this month. They wasted a lot of time hunting for a very slippery enemy, and the thing they killed was only a minor threat, but the ingenuity of getting into that Fomor's den despite it being so intangible and leaving so few traces makes it a sign of their skill as hunters. Another pack has a laundry list of their successes, and at first glance it's like every enemy just fell into their lap.<br /> <br /> If you were to ask questions in the follow-up, you'd find that the various members of the pack are responsible for guarding various important things and are plugged into various mentor-mentee relationships with vital Garou in the Sept. So when there's an easy win to be had, they hear about it first. And, to their credit, they're handling these informal missions very well, and it's not that they're doing anything wrong per se, but it means that Garou who aren't plugged into these mentorships and receiving the right 'tips', the ones who don't have family in every important institution in the town or city or state, lag behind.<br /> <br /> The more frequently you get opportunities to prove yourself, the faster you can ascend. It's also an opportunity to mess up and lose Renown of course, but the more support you have the less likely that is to happen. It might be worth, when writing Rank 4 and 5 NPCs, to consider who got to the top through an easy path, a relatively steady career with victory after victory lining up neatly for them, and who suffered ups and downs with hardships and punishments and chastisement for messing up. It might reflect the themes of the chronicle or the personality of the character, along with their origins and circumstances.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Wisdom</b></u><br /> <br /> For a Garou's career as a Theurge, nothing beats a Theurge mentor. If you're basically the personal assistant of the Master of the Rite then the busywork you're stuck with will, luckily for you, build up your Wisdom faster than expected. Learning Rites grants you Wisdom, riddle contests with Garou and spirits (more easily accessed at a friendly Caern) grant you Wisdom, taking part in purifications (a likely task for a junior Theurge assisting the right people via Mentor and similar) grants Wisdom.<br /> <br /> Essentially, Wisdom is most rapidly accumulated by being at the beck and call of the most important ritualists in a Sept. Hands-on experience with learning and performing Rites will do a lot to build up your Wisdom, and the faster you gain Wisdom the faster you'll gain Rank, which justifies you getting involved in more senior Theurge business.<br /> <br /> It's also important to note that a very hefty Wisdom bonus comes from a threat being defeated without a single Garou coming to 'serious' harm. This is where being in a pack such as one described in the Glory section becomes very relevant. With the right equipment, the right allies, the right planning, the right preparation and support, chances are you'll be striking your prey in situations where they don't get past the ambush round. The turn economy, for an enemy or even a pack of enemies, of a whole Pack of Garou suddenly appearing out of nowhere and all using their Rage for bonus attacks while you're still surprised, is very hard to survive. I think any good ST would be very careful with the 5 point Wisdom reward for 'ending a threat with no serious harm to a Garou', since it basically has the chance to, depending on the story, grant you a full Wisdom gain every single in-game Moot, so long as you have two encounters in the whole month!<br /> <br /> Still, taken as RAW, Wisdom can be very easy to gain if you're in with the right people, and handed the right targets, and could be very difficult to gain if you have to scrounge for favours to learn Rites or be given leads on potential hunts.<br /> <br /> If for some reason you need to top up your temporary Wisdom before a moot swings around, finding and solving problems in your Kinfolk community is a great option. Naturally this requires you to have a Kinfolk community you're part of! But assuming that you have access to such homesteads and communities, you may well have disputes to mediate, matches to suggest, arguments to defuse, and Garou-appropriate jobs to fulfil. Keeping your Kinfolk sweet may well be a good supplementary source of Renown, and is probably easier for Garou who were raised in the Tribe, rather than being 'fallow' until the time of their Change, such as with wolves and humans who were away from the 'fold'.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Honour</b></u><br /> <br /> This is the hardest to quantify. Killing a Fomor, that's very substantial. But keeping your promises immediately brings in some ambiguity. How big a deal does it have to be? Ultimately the more immersed and integrated you are into the culture of the Sept, the more of a common face you are at the gathering places of the Garou in your Tribe or Caern, the more opportunities you will have to gain Honour. If you're the child of an Elder and you have your dinner with honoured Kinfolk and Garou family each night, chances are you will find yourself more often in situations where you need to make and uphold promises, where you overhear things which you are required by honour to inform your Elders of, and where there are disputes to mediate.<br /> <br /> With all that said, the single best way to gather Honour may well be to teach other Garou, which is where your Backgrounds become relatively key. If your Mentor has an important teaching role within the community, it will likely fall to you to help them teach others. If you have been raised in the Garou Nation, learning the responsibilities of Kinfolk and Garou from when you could talk, chances are there will be much you can teach ignorant new cubs, much you can teach your packmates, much you can instruct others of as you go up in Rank. A clever careerist who takes on a difficult mentee at the first opportunity not only looks like a model citizen, they also get ahold of a potential Renown farm as they have more opportunities to teach another, uphold the Litany, give advice and so on.<br /> <br /> Naturally this is assuming it works. If your student is disobedient and willful, and causes trouble, there's every chance it'll reflect poorly on you. So wise advice from a canny political animal would have you pick a compliant or desperate pupil who knows they're on thin ice. For example, someone from a more marginal family, or who lacks any of the connections that power players in the Sept tend to have. Perhaps someone who is a poor fit for their Auspice and knows they need a lot of help to get into the right 'shape' for their job.<br /> <br /> It's important to remember that while we, as players, can read the Renown table and get a great idea of what rewards Renown, PCs, especially Rank 1 PCs who are recently changed Lupus, or recently changed Homids who grew up outside the Nation, won't know IC what will get them Renown. A bit of meta-gaming is likely to happen there without anyone realizing, but NPCs certainly won't have a clue unless someone tells them. While everyone will eventually get clued in by watching the Rite of Accomplishment, which includes recitations of your Honourable deeds, a Garou with the right family and mentoring will likely get a pretty big head start!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-12T13:23:07-07:00","id":1549200,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-size:14px\"><u><b>Challenges</b></u></span><br /> <br /> Getting enough Permanent Renown to rank up is one thing. Think of that like padding your CV. Short of legendary acts which make your promotion basically a given, all Garou who want to go up in Rank need to be ready to undergo Challenges to prove themselves.<br /> <br /> W20 actually does a good job of explaining the difference between the Ranks in this regard. It's possible to be an exceptional Cliath for your Auspice but not quite have what it takes to go up to Fostern, let alone Adren, since you're missing out on some key stuff. This is more and more the case as you go up the Ranks. If you want to make the setting feel cohesive and like it 'adds up', feel free to have Rank 2, Rank 3 and Rank 4 NPCs who have more than enough Renown to go up to the next Rank, but who simply haven't done so.<br /> <br /> They might be waiting for certain Elders to die (not in a mean way!) before they seek ascension, or they might have political reasons to stay where they are, or they've never felt a calling to reach the highest Ranks. There are roles taken on at the highest Ranks which involve much more stress, responsibility, and risk of failure. While there are Garou who are career politicians, political animals, I would wager that the default are those who are fully bought in and who would never seek to be promoted unless they genuinely thought they were bringing something to the table that the Sept needed, at least when you get beyond Rank 3. Adren might be the 'natural ceiling' for many Garou, Athro is much more involved.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Fostern</b></u><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Acquiring the rank of Fostern means that the Garou has grown from a raw recruit into a seasoned veteran. These werewolves have some familiarity with Garou society, and they have learned the nature of the war they are part of. Suitable challenges for this Rank should be moderately difficult - they should test the challenger, not overwhelm her.<br /> <br /> \u2022 \u201cAcquire\u201d a specific item hidden by a Ragabash of appropriate Rank.<br /> \u2022 Receive a boon from a minor spirit.<br /> \u2022 Convince the challenged Garou through impassioned speech and debate to recognize your Rank.<br /> \u2022 Compose a song, poem, or tale honoring the challenged Garou.<br /> \u2022 Defeat an Ahroun of appropriate Rank in a formal duel.<br /> \u2022 Stand guard at the caern for seventy-two hours without falling asleep. </div> </div> </div>My takeaway is that while reaching Rank 2 isn't exactly 'easy', it's also expected that you will do so when you're no longer a 'raw recruit'. Fostern are Garou who are familiar with the Nation and generally have gotten past the stage of asking questions constantly. The example Challenges are pretty pedestrian and are probably easier than whatever you did to get the Renown to be ready to rank up. This one is kind of a rubber stamp. If you are a Galliard and you're unable to come up with a decent piece of prose honouring the Elder you're asking for elevation, I'm a little worried.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Adren</b></u><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Those that reach the Rank of Adren have taken on major responsibilities within their sept. They hold most of the positions of consequence, such as Warder, Master of the Rite, and Gatekeeper (among others). <b>Challenges for this Rank should be designed to weed out those who can\u2019t act quickly or think on their feet</b>; these are, after all, the future leaders of the sept and the tribe. <b>Very few Garou pass their Adren challenge the first time</b>.<br /> <br /> \u2022 Borrow a fetish from the challenged using body language, gestures, and non-verbal cues to ask for it.<br /> \u2022 Finish a difficult scavenger hunt within a predefined time.<br /> \u2022 Create an original (and useful) fetish from scratch within a certain amount of time.<br /> \u2022 Make peace with an enemy or negotiate a truce between two feuding werewolves.<br /> \u2022 Find an obscure hero of the Garou and create a fitting tribute.<br /> \u2022 Withstand severe beatings or taunting from your sept mates without frenzying. </div> </div> </div>Rank 3, Adren, means 'major responsibility'. This means that the nature of the Challenge ought to test for those who are ready to take on major responsibilities, and a wise Garou coming up with a Challenge should be sure to pick something which challenges the Garou appropriately. If the character, or player, goes &quot;ah great, that's easy&quot; it should be because the task is deceptively difficult, not because they've been asked to do something they know they're great at. Challenges with intricate wordings which contain potential tricks and loopholes might be wise.<br /> <br /> I would point out that the difficulties of the suggested challenges here vary wildly. Going into Lupus and using your tail and ears and pointing with a paw to try and get something is pretty easy, I imagine a Cub could pull that off. Making peace between two feuding Werewolves could be anything from difficult to impossible. Regardless, the Challenge really needs to be individualized at this point, and like the writeup says, it should be time-limited and focus on checking to see if the Garou is incapable of being clever or reacting to new problems. Some of the examples are pretty awful at that however.<br /> <br /> Given that reaching Rank 3 means taking on enough responsibility that you will have significant numbers of &quot;underlings&quot;, i.e. basically every Cliath in the Tribe will be bound by the law to obey reasonable orders, I believe that the best Challenges for Adren would involve having to get some Cubs or Cliath to achieve a goal. Delegating under time pressure means you need to identify the skills of your underlings and work with those skills to the best of your ability. Ideally such challenges would also include a curve ball where things change unexpectedly and the Challenged Garou has to quickly instruct their team (perhaps played by the other players!) to take on a different approach.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Athro</b></u><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Athro have risen very high in the hierarchy of the Garou. They are mighty warriors, renowned lorekeepers, wise leaders, notorious tricksters, and famous judges. Werewolves that want a place among this elite group need to demonstrate exceptional skill and intelligence, and those very rare Garou who manage to attain this Rank on the first try are held in awe even by their peers.<br /> <br /> \u2022 Uncover every aspect of the business plan of an organization of potential interest, and do it without getting caught.<br /> \u2022 Restore a lost treasure of the tribe or sept.<br /> \u2022 Convince a spirit to act in a fashion completely contrary to its nature without harming it.<br /> \u2022 Talk a local street gang (or similar group) into a month of community service.<br /> \u2022 Write a song praising a rival, and use it to end your differences with him.<br /> \u2022 Rescue a Garou captured by powerful enemies of some sort, such Black Spiral Dancers, Pentex teams, vampires, or magi. </div> </div> </div>Unfortunately the distinction between Adren and Athro isn't as clear here. Reaching Rank 3 has a key discriminating component of needing to be ready to think on your feet and change your strategy in emergencies. We're told that Garou reaching this rank need to show 'exceptional skill and intelligence' but we aren't given much heft to this concept. Are all Athro and Elder Garou 'intelligent'? I'll let you be the judge.<br /> <br /> To my taste it's very important that tests for ascending to Athro are designed to show that Garou's readiness to handle great affairs which are important to the Sept, and to the wider region of allied Septs. The examples are just very difficult low-level tasks for the most case, which is to be distinguished from higher-level tasks which relate to whole Caerns, local Tribe networks and so on. So a Challenge for Athro might be 'get these two Garou/Kinfolk families to sit down to dinner together', 'bring a whole Shame of Ronin back to Gaia', or 'leave clues to your whereabouts which cannot be solved by the Cliath and Fostern within the time limit, but the Adren manage to overcome with some difficulty'.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Elder</b></u><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The most difficult challenges await those who seek the Rank of Elder. Elders are at the heart of a sept or tribe, and they hold the fate of the Garou Nation in their hands. Their decisions guide the Garou. Their deeds are held up as examples for younger werewolves to follow. Their mistakes can cause severe harm to Gaia, the Garou, or the war against the Wyrm. <b>These kinds of challenges should test every quality the werewolf has</b>.<br /> <br /> \u2022 Visit a caern of each tribe without being noticed, recognized, or challenged, and bring back proof of each visit.<br /> \u2022 Locate a lost caern and convince a totem spirit to adopt it.<br /> \u2022 Redeem, cleanse, and rename a powerful Bane without using Gifts (other than Spirit Speech).<br /> \u2022 Find a Garou lost in Harano and reclaim him.<br /> \u2022 Destroy a powerful Wyrm artifact.<br /> \u2022 Lead a group against a major Wyrm creature or other enemy, and defeat it without losing any of your comrades. </div> </div> </div>At this point Challenges should be holistic. While you have to embody your Auspice, you should be ready to show passing familiarity with the work of all five faces of the moon. An Elder Ahroun needs to be ready to sing the song of her coming victory, carefully weigh up which Packs of Garou shall follow her, entreat the right spirit to lead the hunt (acting through a Theurge if necessary) and come up with a cunning plan to complete the hunt with great tact. At least that's how I see it!<br /> <br /> Most of the examples listed are okay, so long as you assume there is enough context around it to make it a true challenge. The Elder concocting the challenge shouldn't just be watching for victory, but also how the would-be Elder reaches victory. To my taste this should be the only Rank where it's possible to complete the challenge you are set but to be found lacking in your execution and told to try again later. How a Garou responds to this is in itself a test; a mistake made by an Elder is far worse than a mistake made by any other Garou, and so it makes sense that a Garou must be ready to show patience when it is required of them. <hr /><br /> So, do we learn much about Renown and Rank from the process of Challenging? Well one thing is that bias will inevitably creep in, if no one is guarding closely against it. Even if a Garou doesn't mean to play favourites, and they really shouldn't, it's possible for someone to give a test they know someone will shine in, even if they can't be certain they'll succeed, because they are invested in their career, or perhaps their ascent would help them politically.<br /> <br /> By equal measure it's possible for a Challenge-giver to give a Challenge which is harder than it should be; the examples given do vary in difficulty quite greatly! This might be done to hamper the progress of a pawn of a political rival, or out of sheer dislike, or bigotry. That said, it's likely that any examples of this kind of discrimination, or favouritism, in Challenges would need to be minor enough that there is plausible deniability, in most cases. You can't <i>look</i> like you're being unfair, so you'd need to play your cards close to your chest.<br /> <br /> That said, it's important to note that a Garou can challenge <b>any</b> Garou who is of the appropriate Rank. A Rank 2 Bone Gnawer Metis isn't going to bring their Challenge to a Rank 4 Fianna Homid unless they're incredibly ballsy and looking to prove a point. Chances are they're going to bring their Challenge to a Rank 3 Bone Gnawer who they know is a 'good sort' and will challenge them appropriately. This massively undercuts the possibility of an unfair Challenge, but it does increase the likelihood of a Challenge which is too easy. As I understand it, it would be very easy for Garou to pick their mentor, or a relative, or someone who has good reason to play favourites. It's possible to say that this is illegal in the Sept, or that the Elder Council of a given Sept chooses who you should challenge, or that the individual you challenge is chosen randomly, or by the direction of a Rite.<br /> <br /> But if you're going to run it as W20 lays it out, the simplest thing to say is that Garou of various Auspices and Tribes and Breeds are keeping a close eye on proceedings. A Rank 4 Metis who achieves their Rank and begins helping other Metis to climb the ranks at an unreasonable speed won't stay at that Rank for long. A Silver Fang who is showing huge preferential treatment to their Tribe may wind up in trouble with their own kin, as they risk embarrassing the Tribe by bringing unworthy youngsters up to a greater Rank than they can handle. Ultimately, if the social element of the game is well written, it should be an unusual situation if someone feels they can show favour to those who Challenge them without some kind of special leverage or unique situation.<br /> <br /> With that in mind, a Sept where a powerful Garou has such a cult of personality going that they've gotten away with bringing some Garou up to Rank 3 and 4 who really should still be at Rank 2 could make for an interesting subplot in a game. Similarly showing a Sept where Challenges are handled centrally, and where this has resulted in Metis and certain Tribes being left at lower Ranks even more than in a typical Sept, could be a good plot hook for a Philodox looking to get into the weeds of how running a Sept works. The Challenging system looks like it should work, essentially, but there's enough room for it to go wrong that you can get some good stories and Sept concepts out of it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-12T13:52:47-07:00","id":1549204,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1549137-renown-rank-a-deep-dive?p=1549185#post1549185\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm going off the fact that the word 'threat' inherently comes with a POV.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's flimsy at best. Yes, that leaves a lot of room for the ST and table to discuss and adjust the Renown system for their games to make it fun, it doesn't at all mesh with the books statements on how temporary Renown is acquired once the OOC subjectivity is settled. <br /> <br /> By the books, it doesn't matter what different Garou think about what constitutes a threat, the force handing out Renown's ideals win because Renown gets meted out before the Garou can debate anything.<br /> <br /> This conversation is also getting increasingly frustrating because it feels like you want to tell me how things are, instead of actually listening to what I'm saying, addressing it, and having a real discussion. I asked you to point to passages in the book that directly support your position on this, and you just resort of, &quot;well 'threat' is vague word,&quot; as if that somehow proves that Red Talons should get more Glory for killing humans that aren't direct threats, and Get should get Glory for killing Sidhe just for existing. The books clearly state that Glory rewards are adjusted for the potency of the enemy. It never states that Tribal enmity against specific targets adjusts it.<br /> <br /> And I'm backing up what I'm saying with material from the game. The fact that the books felt the need to print a Rite for the Red Talons so they could avoid punishment from the other Tribes for overdoing the killing of humans is clear evidence that the Red Talons know they would lose Renown for what they did if called to task by the rest of the Nation (because culling humans isn't in the Renown system as a knock on one's Renown, but the Red Talons are violating Garou laws by acting against agreed upon terms of engagement).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And if that became a pattern across Garou society across many generations, that Garou who destroy these 'threats' don't get their flowers, then the various Auspices would likely start reading into it. </div> </div> </div><b>That's exactly what happened</b>. Modern Garou don't sing of the Wars of Rage, Shame, the Imperigum or all of that as good things. They are tales of inglorious, dishonorable, and unwise decisions that have cost the Garou so dearly they have to be constantly brought up to remind everyone not to keep repeating the same damn mistakes over and over again.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> While I could understand if someone came up with a different interpretation of what we're presented with, to me it's hard to imagine a world where the Garou didn't get plenty of Glory from all their worst mistakes. I think it feeds very well into the tragedy and horror of the setting if the careers of many great and mighty Elders has their careers built on fat piles of Glory (and to a lesser extent Honour and Wisdom) gained from terrible mistakes that the Garou Nation is paying for to this day. </div> </div> </div>This is circular reasoning. You're putting the conclusion you want before what the books actually say.<br /> <br /> As you are very clearly aware of from the post between the one I'm replying to and this one, Renown farming is not hard to do in-universe for the Garou, and is drastically unfair in access to such methods. Garou don't get Renown for what their ancestors did, they get it for what they do. Their ancestors can only do their best to make sure their bloodlines have easy access to Renown opportunities. Living Elders sitting pretty on fat piles of Glory might benefit from the past mistakes of the Garou, but those piles still have to come from the actions those specific Elders did themselves. You can't inherent Renown. The Garou Nation's ability to be allegory and satire applied to generation wealth disparities is weak because Garou still have to merit Renown even if opportunities aren't fairly divided. On top of that, challenges are a part of Garou culture here too. An Ahroun that's risng the Ranks because of a well coordinated effort to ensure their success can easily lack the personal skills to win challenges on the mound during Moots. Coddling in this fashion is both antithetical to the presented warrior ethos of the Garou Nation, and impractical because such weakness is easily exploited by rivals within the Nation.<br /> <br /> And frankly I find your admitted lack of imagination here dull, not thematic. It's lazy to just have the Elders sitting on top without actual accomplishments to their names riding on past events from generations ago. The setting and themes are more intense and worth exploring when the Elders had to get to the top through real work even if they had advantages along the way. The Elders did things of value and worth to the PCs, even if they're out of touch, obsessed with trivial matters, or whatever. They are proven leaders, and they can be challenged by other Elders and Athros that want their seats at the table, so they have to at least stay sharp against that. All this means Elders are complex potential antagonists because they have to be great, and mighty, and back it up on demand.<br /> <br /> And beyond even that, your whole approach seems to (a bit selectively) treat the Garou as Renown min-maxing robots where Renown is the be all and end all of their motivations. An inidividual Garou that wants a Fetish can care more about the prestige of the Fetish such that they do some underhanded things that cost them all the Renown they would have gotten earning it fairly. A Tribe can want a Cearn for the value it provides even if the Renown rewards are a pittance or even negative after forcing it out of the hands of those currently taking care of it. The whole reason Garou history is so full of FUBAR situations, is because Garou do things without thinking about the Renown. That's why there's enough Crinos-born alive to be playable. They crave more than validation via Renown, and the interplay between personal desires and Renown is fodder for stories for decades of play.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If you're trying to figure out why this is the case from an in-universe perspective, the easiest explanation would be that the spirits differ (different ancestors, different broods as audiences) or that the Garou differ (Garou follow their leaders, and the Renown you're receiving reflects the values of your Elders by default). </div> </div> </div>Or you just don't bother. If you've been playing a single game that long, your characters have more than enough Renown to play around with to explore the impact of changing attitudes with the impersonal impact of Renown. If you're not doing one really long game and starting new Chronicles, you just play the game differently. They don't require continuity with each other.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-12T15:20:35-07:00","id":1549211,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><b>Please keep the discussion civil, avoid personal attacks and inflammatory remarks about how another table runs their game. Thanks! </b></span>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/They<br /> Moderator<br /> Line Developer: <b><a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/browse/pub/4261/Onyx-Path-Publishing/subcategory/8329_24824/Scarred-Lands-new\" target=\"_blank\">Scarred Lands</a></b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25411-travislegge","name":"TravisLegge","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25411_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"227"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-13T06:00:14-07:00","id":1549231,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar11620_5.jpg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"11620\" data-vbnamecard=\"11620\">twig</a>, your article clearly provides ample food for thought and I appreciate your willingness to share your theories of Renown, as applied and policed by the Garou.<br /> <br /> I also wanted to point out that your critiques of various Rites of Punishment seems to be purely grounded in the geopolitical &amp; social situation of Modern Nights - where the Garou are never more than a dance away from the Apocalypse.<br /> <br /> It seems only fair to point out that, back in the Dark Ages and comparable eras (When Garou and Gaia were doing better) Rites like The Hunt were probably created because there were far fewer chances to send a Garou to perish in honourable combat against Creatures of the Wyrm - if only because creatures of the Wyrm potent enough to fell a Garou would have almost certainly been targeted by local packs, leaving few other possibilities for an honourable demise that did not involve some risk of breaching the Veil.<br /> <br /> It also seems reasonable to point out that The Rite of the Omega is evidence in favour of the notion that while \u2018Right makes Might\u2019 in Garou society (with a particular emphasis on individual might), there have always been means by which the over-mighty can be taken down a peg or two by collective action, methods fully-approved of by Gaia and the Spirit World, even.<br /> <br /> Also, I find it difficult to agree that the Stone of Scorn is wholly pointless or disproportionate when our own reality obliges us to daily endure wealthy, powerful, prestigious individuals who really, truly and richly deserve it.<br /> <br /> If nothing else, it bears considering that this Rite was clearly out together for the benefit of the community, not the condemned individual - being allowed to sit around and just talk smack about some malefactor for hours at a time with your friends, colleagues &amp; family must be extremely therapeutic, even (or perhaps especially) in the Age of the Apocalypse, though one trusts it would be a fairly rare luxury by this point.<br /> <br /> Anyway, doubtless you\u2019ll be able to conjure up counterpoints, but it bears pointing out that the Garou National is built not on institutional, but on interpersonal politics for the excellent reason that (with perhaps no more than 11,000 Garou in the world as of AD 1992) there would be be a very real difficulty in constructing a system of impersonal justice when just about every single party in every single case brought before a sept\u2019s elders would involve persons who have either known each other all their days as a Garou or have quite literally known each other all their days and will almost certainly have worked, fought and hunted together).<br /> <br /> That\u2019s a way of life arguably more alien to our lived experience than any Honour culture (Since there\u2019s a very strong case to be made that \u2018cancelling\u2019 and similar institutions of the Digital Age are merely the latest manifestations of very ancient impulses).<br /> <br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar3046_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"3046\" data-vbnamecard=\"3046\">Black Fox</a>, I also wanted to push back a little against your disbelief in the notion that the Spirit World would pay much mind to the average Garou - given the rarity and raw power of even a fairly young, untested Garou (and their nature as a melding of spirit &amp; flesh) it makes perfect sense that they\u2019d draw the attention of local spirits the way Classical tragedies produced a Greek Chorus (and for almost exactly the same reasons, Garou being in many ways Heroes from a Mythic Age fighting on long after their age went up in flames).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28339-atticus-cleanclaw","name":"Atticus Cleanclaw","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"638"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-13T06:08:53-07:00","id":1549232,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1549137-renown-rank-a-deep-dive?p=1549183#post1549183\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It would have been nice if, at some point over WtA's history, they'd actually done some examples of local Litanies to help elaborate on that point. I get that books have other priorities, but the Rage Across the [Insert Area] books would have likely been improved by some pages dedicated towards what the local Litany/Litanies look like in more detail. It's great for the whole game line to see that in practice, and great for helping the authors do some local world building by doing the thought experiments of what has shaped local Garou laws in specific directions and precedents. It's certainly not a topic the various ST books ever really gave the love it needed to help STs craft it themselves either; so some examples would have at least helped.<br /> <br /> It's also good for the Galliards because local laws come from local history. A player that struggles with coming up with tales to sing beyond the pack's exploits or similar prompts can have a slew of prompts in weird local laws and how they came about. A good reason not to get too detailed unless it's important so the players can add their own creative spin on things.</div> </div> </div> </div>I wonder if the best way to produce a \u2018sourcebook\u2019 on this sort of thing would be to create a series of adventures that puts PCs into various criminal cases and requires them to sleuth, lawyer and likewise work their way through a number of disparate Sept cultures? (Make them troubleshooters for some elder Philodox - possibly a Shadow Lord, so that there\u2019s an option for the Storyteller to make the Elder\u2019s own political ambitions and intrigues a crucial subplot or even the engine driving the campaign).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28339-atticus-cleanclaw","name":"Atticus Cleanclaw","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"638"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-13T06:11:18-07:00","id":1549233,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28339-atticus-cleanclaw\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"28339\" data-vbnamecard=\"28339\">Atticus Cleanclaw</a> appreciate the response! Nodding along reading this, easy to forget that these exact same rites were in use in 1000 AD and 1000 BC and far beyond that. There will probably be Rites which are used much less now than they once were because Homids have changed a bit, and the time pressures have changed. Like you say, devoting a whole evening to one Punishment Rite might be a rare luxury nowadays.<br /> <br /> The benefits of the Rite of the Omega Wolf allowing a pack to basically group-defeat their corrupt or failing Alpha are actually great, and it's definitely something I skimmed over. Even if the Alpha's only crime is &quot;he's a toxic bully&quot;, which I don't believe most of the Garou Nation would care about (at least based on my reading of Changing Ways), the overthrow will be seen as just since the Ritual taking place is proof that the leader was a toxic <i>failing</i> bully, one who failed to keep his whole pack in line.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-13T06:12:16-07:00","id":1549234,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1549137-renown-rank-a-deep-dive?p=1549186#post1549186\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Yeah nodding along with this. I think that the idea of Garou being observed by spirits at all times kiiiiiiiiiinda works, if it's explained in greater depth and the writers give a clear answer, instead of going &quot;maybe this, maybe that&quot;. If the main role of certain Ancestors is just to observe Garou of their Tribe and reward/punish their behaviour, that could make sense. It might even make it a potential plot point that you think you could find a killer by traveling to your Ancestral Homeland in the Umbra and questioning your Ancestors.<br /> <br /> But it leaves me feeling like... if the spirit world is so omniscient, despite the thick Gauntlet which is getting thicker as years go by, why aren't they sharing all that intel? They have every reason to. As an explanation it feels a bit hollow, unless it were very carefully considered and written by a writer and/or game designer better than me by far.<br /> <br /> And yeah, the issue of a fair game in an unfair setting is always really important. Bone Gnawers getting mistreated by and large shouldn't make it so that your BG PC lags behind the others in Rank. Hitting that balance is very, very hard! I'm reminded of how in Skyrim racism is a big 'theme' (though using that word feels like it's giving the writers too much credit), but your character is basically racially agnostic. No one cares whether you're a Nord or a Dunmer or an Argonian, including the guy who spends his days shouting slurs at Dunmer or the people who keep Dunmer stuck in a little ghetto. The disconnect between narrative and mechanics is so sharp that it feels like your character is a visitor from another world, or that they have some special pass stuck to their armour. There's definitely a balance that can be struck.</div> </div> </div> </div> It bears pointing out that bigots are perfectly capable of making exceptions to their \u2018rules\u2019 and that there\u2019s nothing more blatantly-exceptional than somebody who can SHOUT DRAGONS TO DEATH.<br /> <br /> No, I didn\u2019t have to put the last bit in All Caps, but it did seem appropriate.<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28339-atticus-cleanclaw","name":"Atticus Cleanclaw","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"638"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-13T07:00:06-07:00","id":1549236,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1549137-renown-rank-a-deep-dive?p=1549233#post1549233\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28339-atticus-cleanclaw\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"28339\" data-vbnamecard=\"28339\">Atticus Cleanclaw</a> appreciate the response! Nodding along reading this, easy to forget that these exact same rites were in use in 1000 AD and 1000 BC and far beyond that. There will probably be Rites which are used much less now than they once were because Homids have changed a bit, and the time pressures have changed. Like you say, devoting a whole evening to one Punishment Rite might be a rare luxury nowadays.<br /> <br /> The benefits of the Rite of the Omega Wolf allowing a pack to basically group-defeat their corrupt or failing Alpha are actually great, and it's definitely something I skimmed over. Even if the Alpha's only crime is &quot;he's a toxic bully&quot;, which I don't believe most of the Garou Nation would care about (at least based on my reading of Changing Ways), the overthrow will be seen as just since the Ritual taking place is proof that the leader was a toxic <i>failing</i> bully, one who failed to keep his whole pack in line.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think that talking about \u201cmost of the Garou Nation\u201d is also a risky thing to do, given that the Nation not only can be, but is, so very disparate, so very diffused across a big world and so diverse (Given the mix of Homid, Lupus and \u2018Metis\u2019, even before we get into the different tribal and Homid cultures).<br /> <br /> If nothing else, it bears pointing out again that so much of Garou society is intensely personal - a sept might willing endure Bites Low, the swaggering bully Ahroun who also happens to have saved the lives of every Garou in the local pack twice over by being very big, very mean, very fearless and very lucky (The very qualities which make him so very unloveable in day-to-day existence), but gleefully condemn some knockabout who swaggers into the caern and starts acting like he owns it, whether he\u2019s well along the path to producing the next Jonas Albrecht or just happens to be some new kid who hasn\u2019t worked out that acting big doesn\u2019t make you someone (It further bears pointing out that The Rite of Boasting is somewhat misnamed - it might more accurately be described as \u2018The Rite of Promise\u2019 because you are making some serious commitments that only become mere boasting if you fail to deliver on them - hit your target and it becomes more a case of \u2018Calling your shots\u2019).<br /> <br /> Anyway, thank you again for giving us all so much to chew over - I suspect that there would be absolutely no debate on this thread were the subject and the articles in it so worthy of interest (In all honesty, I\u2019d bet money that Philodoxes in particular and Garou from across the nation have been arguing much the same topics, albeit from a very different perspective, for as long as the Litany and the Garou Nation has existed).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":28339,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28339-atticus-cleanclaw","user":"Atticus Cleanclaw","date":".  Small punctuation error 03-17-2025, 12:12 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28339-atticus-cleanclaw","name":"Atticus Cleanclaw","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"638"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-13T11:55:42-07:00","id":1549247,"title":"","content":"Yeah I can easily imagine a debate of &quot;DID RAIDING PARTIES IN THE WAR OF RAGE GET GLORY RENOWN (NO FLAMES)&quot; on the Glass Walker umbranet forums going on for 500 increasingly aggressive posts before being banned by moderator-spirits.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-13T11:58:06-07:00","id":1549248,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Atticus Cleanclaw</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1549137-renown-rank-a-deep-dive?p=1549232#post1549232\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I wonder if the best way to produce a \u2018sourcebook\u2019 on this sort of thing would be to create a series of adventures that puts PCs into various criminal cases and requires them to sleuth, lawyer and likewise work their way through a number of disparate Sept cultures? (Make them troubleshooters for some elder Philodox - possibly a Shadow Lord, so that there\u2019s an option for the Storyteller to make the Elder\u2019s own political ambitions and intrigues a crucial subplot or even the engine driving the campaign).</div> </div> </div> </div>There really isn't a &quot;best&quot; approach here because different people absorb what they need about the setting in different ways, and then there's the whole reality of what it takes to make a gaming book suitable for publishing; esp. one focused on a specific topic.<br /> <br /> I, personally, don't believe a Sept-jumping criminal investigation team premade chronicle would do that much on its own. It's a fairly niche product that a lot of people aren't going to play, and thus it's not going to become part of their WtA experience to carry forward into how they approach the game when making their own settings and content.<br /> <br /> Ideally, the whole game-line would take this into account instead of it feeling like a throw-away line present in all the core-books and maybe getting a sentence or two of callback in a Players Guide or ST book. That doesn't mean every book needs 10 pages of material on the subject of local law variations, but it just coming up more frequently as part of navigating Garou society beyond interacting with your Pack and Sept. Specific to the subject of this thread broadly, something like a sample Rank challenge of, &quot;Convince the Sept to adopt a local law from another Sept.&quot; (feels hard Adren to mid Athro to me, but that's a larger issue). When these sorts of small entries are present in most books, then books that focus more on travel or specific locations have more impact because &quot;what are the local laws in these Septs?&quot; is something players are primed to want to know instead of an obstacle that is suddenly thrust in their path if they forgot about that one line in a massive tome of a book.<br /> <br /> If that's already in place adequately, then a book about being a traveling trouble shooter pack with a sample adventure can have the kind of impact you're suggesting. If it's not already in place, then it's usually more productive to have a book that's more of a topic guide that's meant to fill in details (and yes, please anyone that reads this and immediately decides to go to the STV and make such of book, fill it with lots of plot hooks and so on so it's not just essays on legal stuff) that the core book(s) cut for whatever reason.<br /> <br /> To point to an example from a different 20th line: The Rich Bastard's Guide to Magick. Mages and money has always sorta been alluded to in the games (either groups that want to use their powers to cease to have to worry about material needs, or mages that eschew such systems) - even with a whole Union Convention centered around finance - but never really had any clear prompts for it being something that matters beyond concepts that make it matter. Putting out a whole supplement on money and magic can prompt people to reconsider if they want to have this come up more in their games and how just because it is suddenly a topic worthy of a whole book.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-13T12:00:55-07:00","id":1549249,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Atticus Cleanclaw</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1549137-renown-rank-a-deep-dive?p=1549231#post1549231\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I also wanted to push back a little against your disbelief in the notion that the Spirit World would pay much mind to the average Garou - given the rarity and raw power of even a fairly young, untested Garou (and their nature as a melding of spirit &amp; flesh) it makes perfect sense that they\u2019d draw the attention of local spirits the way Classical tragedies produced a Greek Chorus (and for almost exactly the same reasons, Garou being in many ways Heroes from a Mythic Age fighting on long after their age went up in flames).</div> </div> </div> </div>No, I heavily disagree. It doesn't work that from a mechanical perspective, and it would be bad from a narrative perspective.<br /> <br /> As I had previously said, it's OK if some spirits observe some things and that information is gossiped around. The occasional spirit is going to observe something at some point. However, spirits are neither omniscient nor omnipresent (either individually or as a collective), and that is what they would need to be in order to act as some kind of objective automatic renown removal and scandal note taker.<br /> <br /> We know several things from the setting. The first is that in order for spirits to peek from the Umbra into the Realm, they would either need to Peek (which means the spirits are not able to observe the Umbra around them) or need a Charm like Realm Sense (or perhaps Materialize). But not all spirits have Realm Sense, and those who do are limited to only that area of the realm to which that spirit is intrinsically linked. Furthermore, that Charm is not on all the time and requires a Gnosis roll to activate. Simply put, spirits are not constantly observing the Realm. And when they do observe the Realm, it's probably not for the purpose of observing Garou. Spirits in fact have other things to do than just observe Garou. Not even pack totems work that way. Even a powerful pack totem is just one spirit who can't shadow every single pack member in the Umbra if the pack members split up. Being able to instantly communicate with them or find them is not the same as knowing as what every single member is doing.<br /> <br /> Second, in the modern day setting we know the Umbra is severely depopulated compared to how it was in the oldest days. There are vast swaths of the Penumbra that are basically denuded of spirits, or just have enemy spirits. Only at caerns and domains like glens are there probably spirits who could potentially be looking in the Realm and observe what a Garou there is doing. It's not even clear if spirits would know all the things Garou have done in the Umbra itself, much less the Realm. Even in places like caerns where we can imagine there are lots of spirits present, and that some of them will be viewing the activities and discussions of the Garou, that one of the spirits will always be able to track every single Garou and know what is going on. Even in parties with dozens of people, it is possible in real life for two or more attendees to go off and have their own private discussion or activity unknown to the rest of the party goers.<br /> <br /> Third, there are Gifts, Rites, and Fetishes whose entire purpose is to conceal what goes on from spirits or banish them outright.<br /> <br /> From a narrative perspective, this idea is awful. An ST could never tell a story about betrayal, crimes, violation of the Litany, and so on because all Garou are constantly being snitched on. And things that are subjective suddenly become objective. It makes for a bad setting. Let's use Rage Across New York as an example.<br /> <br /> Is Mother Larissa in the Sept of the Green such a bad sept leader that she is constantly losing Renown as the spirits tell everyone how she doesn't run regular moots, isn't controlling man eating by Bone Juice (whose crimes of course would be constantly reported upon), making deals with the Sabbat, and otherwise acting like a Bone Gnawer? Or because she runs what is an essentially Bone Gnawer sept, is this OK and her tribe thinks she's doing a great job even if Garou like Zachary Ellison thinks differently? For that matter, how exactly can Ellison attempt to orchestrate a coup and seize control of the caern if the spirits are constantly snitching on him? How can Bone Juice be secretly organizing a group of man eaters? How come nobody knows that Arn Guth Stormbright and Bladetooth are the parents of Aeschylus/Pink Tom? Why isn't Jacob Morningkill being hit with massive Renown loss as he tyrannizes his tribe and unjustly exiles his heir?<br /> <br /> For that matter, how can any Garou possibly become a Black Spiral Dancer if the spirits are constantly observing Garou and can automatically warn everyone else? In fact, why aren't the Black Spiral Dancers and their plans constantly being monitored and exposed? Or the plots of any other Wyrm minions? In Rite of Passage, the Winter Wolf Sept should not just know that their cubs have been killed in New York City, but also know that Black Spiral Dancers did it and that the Garou at the Sept of the Green are innocent. That would invalidate the entire scenario.<br /> <br /> The setting and the game would lose a lot of mystery, intrigue, suspense, secrets, and other dramatic elements that make it fun and interesting.<br /> <br /> The potential for spirits observing what a Garou is doing is present. There's no problem for an ST to decide that something was, in fact, observed, or even that some particular spirit is indeed spying on one or more characters (either because it has individual motivation to do so, or sent by some Theurge, other Garou, or even by a non-Garou). But 1) that should be decided in advance, and 2) smart characters should be able to counter that spying if they so choose.<br /> <br /> The desire for &quot;fairness&quot; is understandable and necessary from a player perspective, but there's no reason to try to force it into an in character perspective by suddenly having the spirit world act in ways completely different from what we've previously been told.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-13T13:17:37-07:00","id":1549257,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar3046_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"3046\" data-vbnamecard=\"3046\">Black Fox</a> to my taste, if you're going to do 'spirits are watching and judging', it should be much more abstract and weird than &quot;spirits in the local penumbra saw you&quot;. It's more that your Totem / Ancestors (either works, but probably pick one and run with it) senses what kind of misdeed you're performing and adjusts your temporary Renown accordingly. The spirits doing so are deep in the Umbra, quasi-omniscient, but bound in ways which make it impractical to show up to your local Master of the Rite and gossip about how you let a Vampire drink your blood.<br /> <br /> Is it messy and a little dumb? Maybe. But I think that there would be ways to make it work if it were workshopped carefully.<br /> <br /> Hell, another angle is that the Garou themselves counts as the witness. Garou are part spirit. Perhaps it is their inner spiritual essence which witnesses the crime and judges them accordingly. We know that Garou have powerful instincts, so an instinctive sense of morality reinforced by 100s of generations of law and punishment and obedience isn't a big push. If I were rewriting the Renown lore I might run with that.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-17T09:15:55-07:00","id":1549391,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1549137-renown-rank-a-deep-dive?p=1549247#post1549247\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yeah I can easily imagine a debate of &quot;DID RAIDING PARTIES IN THE WAR OF RAGE GET GLORY RENOWN (NO FLAMES)&quot; on the Glass Walker umbranet forums going on for 500 increasingly aggressive posts before being banned by moderator-spirits.</div> </div> </div> </div> Or, as RoachBGood.com would call it \u2018Monday\u2019 (Heck, of thing gets that bad when cranky werewolves are NOT in the same room, imagine a handful of Philodoxes or Gaillards bickering like angry Rabbis over the same contentious tradition for the entire span of human history).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28339-atticus-cleanclaw","name":"Atticus Cleanclaw","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"638"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-17T09:19:55-07:00","id":1549392,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1549137-renown-rank-a-deep-dive?p=1549249#post1549249\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> No, I heavily disagree. It doesn't work that from a mechanical perspective, and it would be bad from a narrative perspective.<br /> <br /> As I had previously said, it's OK if some spirits observe some things and that information is gossiped around. The occasional spirit is going to observe something at some point. However, spirits are neither omniscient nor omnipresent (either individually or as a collective), and that is what they would need to be in order to act as some kind of objective automatic renown removal and scandal note taker.<br /> <br /> We know several things from the setting. The first is that in order for spirits to peek from the Umbra into the Realm, they would either need to Peek (which means the spirits are not able to observe the Umbra around them) or need a Charm like Realm Sense (or perhaps Materialize). But not all spirits have Realm Sense, and those who do are limited to only that area of the realm to which that spirit is intrinsically linked. Furthermore, that Charm is not on all the time and requires a Gnosis roll to activate. Simply put, spirits are not constantly observing the Realm. And when they do observe the Realm, it's probably not for the purpose of observing Garou. Spirits in fact have other things to do than just observe Garou. Not even pack totems work that way. Even a powerful pack totem is just one spirit who can't shadow every single pack member in the Umbra if the pack members split up. Being able to instantly communicate with them or find them is not the same as knowing as what every single member is doing.<br /> <br /> Second, in the modern day setting we know the Umbra is severely depopulated compared to how it was in the oldest days. There are vast swaths of the Penumbra that are basically denuded of spirits, or just have enemy spirits. Only at caerns and domains like glens are there probably spirits who could potentially be looking in the Realm and observe what a Garou there is doing. It's not even clear if spirits would know all the things Garou have done in the Umbra itself, much less the Realm. Even in places like caerns where we can imagine there are lots of spirits present, and that some of them will be viewing the activities and discussions of the Garou, that one of the spirits will always be able to track every single Garou and know what is going on. Even in parties with dozens of people, it is possible in real life for two or more attendees to go off and have their own private discussion or activity unknown to the rest of the party goers.<br /> <br /> Third, there are Gifts, Rites, and Fetishes whose entire purpose is to conceal what goes on from spirits or banish them outright.<br /> <br /> From a narrative perspective, this idea is awful. An ST could never tell a story about betrayal, crimes, violation of the Litany, and so on because all Garou are constantly being snitched on. And things that are subjective suddenly become objective. It makes for a bad setting. Let's use Rage Across New York as an example.<br /> <br /> Is Mother Larissa in the Sept of the Green such a bad sept leader that she is constantly losing Renown as the spirits tell everyone how she doesn't run regular moots, isn't controlling man eating by Bone Juice (whose crimes of course would be constantly reported upon), making deals with the Sabbat, and otherwise acting like a Bone Gnawer? Or because she runs what is an essentially Bone Gnawer sept, is this OK and her tribe thinks she's doing a great job even if Garou like Zachary Ellison thinks differently? For that matter, how exactly can Ellison attempt to orchestrate a coup and seize control of the caern if the spirits are constantly snitching on him? How can Bone Juice be secretly organizing a group of man eaters? How come nobody knows that Arn Guth Stormbright and Bladetooth are the parents of Aeschylus/Pink Tom? Why isn't Jacob Morningkill being hit with massive Renown loss as he tyrannizes his tribe and unjustly exiles his heir?<br /> <br /> For that matter, how can any Garou possibly become a Black Spiral Dancer if the spirits are constantly observing Garou and can automatically warn everyone else? In fact, why aren't the Black Spiral Dancers and their plans constantly being monitored and exposed? Or the plots of any other Wyrm minions? In Rite of Passage, the Winter Wolf Sept should not just know that their cubs have been killed in New York City, but also know that Black Spiral Dancers did it and that the Garou at the Sept of the Green are innocent. That would invalidate the entire scenario.<br /> <br /> The setting and the game would lose a lot of mystery, intrigue, suspense, secrets, and other dramatic elements that make it fun and interesting.<br /> <br /> The potential for spirits observing what a Garou is doing is present. There's no problem for an ST to decide that something was, in fact, observed, or even that some particular spirit is indeed spying on one or more characters (either because it has individual motivation to do so, or sent by some Theurge, other Garou, or even by a non-Garou). But 1) that should be decided in advance, and 2) smart characters should be able to counter that spying if they so choose.<br /> <br /> The desire for &quot;fairness&quot; is understandable and necessary from a player perspective, but there's no reason to try to force it into an in character perspective by suddenly having the spirit world act in ways completely different from what we've previously been told.</div> </div> </div> </div> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar3046_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"3046\" data-vbnamecard=\"3046\">Black Fox</a>, please note that I explicitly evoked the Olympians as seen in Classical tragedy, not Almighty God - the example is quite specific, because it points out that it\u2019s perfectly possible (even likely) for the Spirits to be watching and entirely likely that these observers will be as much of problem as a blessing (Possibly even an actively malevolent presence), much like the Twelve Olympians of Ancient Legend.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28339-atticus-cleanclaw","name":"Atticus Cleanclaw","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"638"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-17T12:39:40-07:00","id":1549398,"title":"","content":"I feel like people are approaching this from an all or nothing kinda standpoint. The books also say spirits are the citizenry of the umbra, with their own agendas, interests, and motives. This is repeated across multiple corebooks. My read on that is it's less the reverent attention paid to mythical beings and more like seeing a cousin that got a football scholarship is horsing around with his friends in the backyard. It's less a tell everyone thing and more telling the parents at the table the siblings were fighting again when the topic gets brought up.<br /> <br /> Its important to remember that not all spirits are automatically awake and that's a constant across every edition. In fact, most are asleep and need to be awoken by a Gaian on order to interact with them, and even then it's incredibly brief, the results in the rolls are typically vague and the Garou needs to do a little more work and questioning to find out exactly what happened by asking other spirits. If you've ever been woken up out of REM sleep and asked by someone where a misplaced item in the house is, that's gonna be the disposition of these spirits. Brief, confused, disoriented and only somewhat there.<br /> <br /> They're also not automatically cooperative. Even summoning one comes with a tangible risk of them straight up being immediately hostile if the Garou fails to perform the appropriate chiminage and is another cross-edirion consistency. It's straight up on a rolling table. In fact, you need certain rites or gifts to command spirits, and if you have a reputation of manhandling spirits and not respecting them they have a tendency to become cagey or hostile, just like people. Even a squirrel spirit could wreck a Garou's day if its feeling insulted or the Garou fails to keep a promise. <br /> <br /> Or if you wanna get more practical, spirits witnessing something is like a crime occurring in a neighborhood. Sure, people saw it, or heard it while they were doing something else but also everyone has jobs, or don't trust the cops because they keep roughing up the locals, or maybe that Garou seeking the information is straight up a stranger to the spirits. That's why chiminage and contrition exist (and typically get used liberally) because the spirits have opinions and needs of their own.<br /> <br /> Alright, there is a dead Garou in the umbra in this hypothetical. How did they die? Maybe one spirit saw and is running from the thing that did the killing. Maybe the killing was justified and now the local jagglings are skittish around Garou. The tellurian is way bigger than just the Garou. The Garou are the latecomers to the spirit party and brought into a world that wasn't made for them. Add in a historical tendency for Garou to blow things way out of proportion and maybe spirits aren't exactly Disney buds with every Garou they see. That's the status quo of the penumbra. There is a literal realm for the aforementioned legendary stuff and reverence and it's not on the other side of a mirror. What's on the other side of the mirror is supposed to be a the world reflecting itself, and the world is sick and hurting.<br /> <br /> That is, if the players want to find the killer in this kinda scenario I outline above they gotta put in the leg work. That's like, the game right there. As others say above, if the spirits were always present and always watching, the BSDs wouldn't have gotten as powerful as they are and wouldn't represent nearly the risk they do. In a perfect world, sure, the spirits would be 100% aboard with helping their Gaian brethren, but WtA is definitively an imperfect world where the Gaians have dozens of ways they've wronged the spirits and each other. Golden Rule of Storytelling notwithstanding of course. Fixing and mending social rifts it is literally half the books though.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/448972/Hearthbound-Tribe-Supplement?affiliate_id=4046720\" target=\"_blank\">Hearthbound</a> and Project Director for <a href=\"https://www.tumblr.com/songoftrillium/729204622290550784/likes-dont-increase-visibility-to-others-please\" target=\"_blank\">Werewolf: the Essentials</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83e\udd70","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27167-mundusartis","name":"mundusartis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27167_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"89"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}